up communication service Web4Groups The study contributes to the implementation of these  advanced features through the development of specific design guidelines .As a starting point, a basic conception is  suggested which encompasses  the similarities of voting and rating at  the level of social action. Various examples of rating activities in everyday life illustrate the importance of evaluating objects according to the knowledge, interests, or opinions of individuals, experts, or groups of people. Discussion of different ways of supporting voting activities through telecommunications  highlights relevant aspects of large-scale (in populations) and small-scale (in groups)application. The results of surveys conducted on  voting and rating were the  basis for the design of a Web4Groups voting tool for group decision-making, as well as  a Web4Groups rating tool for enhancing context information in discussion forums.\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\tx3402\adjustright {\lang2057 \par \par }{\fs32\lang2057 Keyword list:\par }{\fs28\lang2057 Voting, rating, computer-mediated communication, user requirements, CSCW, Web4Groups\par }{\lang2057 \par }{\fs28\lang2057 Dissemination level:\tab PU\par Nature:\tab RE\par }{\lang2057 \par }{\fs28\lang2057 Contractual date of delivery:\tab March 1997\par Actual date of delivery:\tab March 1997\par }{\lang2057 \par }{\fs32\lang2057 Executive summary\par }{\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 This report presents the findings of a study investigating the feasibility of voting and rating processes using the group communication service Web4Groups. The study investigates  relevant aspects of the implementation of voting and rating functionalities in Web4Groups and provides first assumptions about the possible consequences. The method applied combines the development of a theoretical framework for rating and vo}{\lang2057 t}{\lang2057 ing processes with a scenario technique for the integration of user needs.\par \par The  study is part of an international project to establish a service for advanced group communication on the WorldWideWeb. The participants  are software deve}{\lang2057 l}{\lang2057 opers, social science researchers and various other groups interested in usi}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 g the service. A constant exchange of ideas  between developers, r}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 searchers and users was  deliberately employed throughout  the project to develop a sy}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 tem which starts from viable needs and passes through sound assessment to become a sustai}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 able service.\par \par The study is marked by two intentions: \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 (1)\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-510\li510\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent510\pnsp227\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}\ls1\adjustright {\lang2057 to analyse the feasibility of transferring some well-known activities of everyday life (voting and rating) to computer-mediated communication contexts, and to  explore completely new ways of performing such activities,\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 (2)\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-510\li510\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent510\pnsp227\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}\ls1\adjustright {\lang2057 to provide guidelines for the design of a voting and a rating tool in Web4Groups.\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par Considering that the support of voting and rating could be an important requirement for future media, our investigations focused on two aspects: on rating activities in decision-making processes (\lquote voting\rquote ) on the one hand, and on the rating of content (\lquote rating\rquote  of webpages, of contributions to a discussion, etc.)on the other hand . \par \par In section 1 a basic conception of voting and rating is developed as a starting point for further inquiries. It is argued that voting and rating are activities that show some b}{\lang2057 a}{\lang2057 sic similarities. The common feature of both activities is the evaluation of objects (e.g. a person, a topic, or the content of a document). Differences between them result from specific procedures and there will be differences in what follows the outcomes. Therefore it is su}{\lang2057 g}{\lang2057 gested that voting and rating can best be understood in terms of actions, conditions and co}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 sequences. \par \par In section 2 some of the numerous forms of rating in everyday life are listed and shortly discussed. People are accustomed  to participate  in rating processes and they  rely on their results in daily information filtering and decision-making. In many situations of ev}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 ryday life people are likely to find themselves in at least one of three roles: as a subject of some  rating activity (e.g. telling a friend about the quality of a book), as  an object of some rating process (e.g. undergoing assessment in school or at work), or to be a part of the audience of the outcome of a rating process (e.g. watching the results of elections on TV). A close look at the great variety of everyday rating activities and their implications should help us to imagine uses which could be supported by computer-mediated communication.\par \par Section 3 deals with voting and telecommunications both on a large and a small scale. First, efforts of tele-democracy and tele-voting are presented which are attempts  to democratise a population's participation in political decision-making through the use of telecommunications. Secondly, it is shown that serious efforts are made to support decision\_making in small groups. In a concluding argument both cases of decision support, i.e. on a large scale and on a small scale, are brought together by dealing with the \lquote mechanics of voting\rquote . Relevant findings from social choice theory are r}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 viewed and used to  determine  the elements necessary in  voting procedures.\par \par In section 4 a side issue of any voting and rating process is discussed, namely how people's preferences or opinions are investigated. A close look at surveying in the social sciences is taken to reflect on the problems of conducting a poll or a survey under conditions of computer-mediated communication.\par \par Finally, section 5 describes guidelines for the development of voting and rating agents for Web4Groups. The technical architecture and the concept of the Web4Groups service are described to explain the integration of voting and rating functio}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 alities. The needs of the participating user groups are analysed in detail to specify their requirements. For voting the actions to be performed by  the agent in the different  phases, from the start to the closing of a vote, are defined. Different models of how ind}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 vidual preferences may be aggregated to arrive at  a collective choice are developed. On the basis of these voting models a syntax for a voting script is suggested. For rating, an agent with a basic functionality is specified allowing for the development of more complex appl}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 cations. A syntax is suggested which complies with current  standards development (PICS). Both for voting and for rating first mock-ups of user interfaces are presented.\par \par The comprehensive appendix includes an elaborate scenario on linking rating with  role organisation in groups, several concrete scenarios of voting and rating contributed  by participating  user groups, as well as  a review of voting and rating ser}{\lang2057 v}{\lang2057 ices already available on the Internet.\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\tx3402\adjustright {\lang2057 \par \page }{\b\lang2057  Contents\par }{\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\tqr\tx8931\adjustright {\lang2057 1. A Basic Conception of Voting and Ra