\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \row }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par Institutionalised rating processes may be found in various forms in everyday life (e.g. as an opinion poll in market research, as a vote or election in a political system, or as an evaluation through a jury). All of them employ a specific method to serve a specific function. \par The functions of rating processes include tailoring of information (e.g. to target groups), decision-making (e.g. on the seats in a committee), or assessment of performance (e.g. awarding of honours). The common methods to achieve ratings can be divided into two main groups: polling and expertise.\par \par }{\b\lang2057 Polling}{\lang2057 : a sample of people or a whole population are asked to provide a rating on a certain object according to their individual opinion.\par \par Polling is a method of collecting information on individual preferences which can take many forms in everyday life. They range from market research on consumer preferences or political preferences to actual decision-making through votes or elections.  In a poll (similar terms: survey, inquiry) the preference schedules of a number of persons are investigated. The unit of analysis can be the whole population of a certain entity (e.g. a group, a city, a nation), a representative sample of the population, or an arbitrary collection of ind}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 viduals. A poll may provide that people express their preferences in terms of choosing one o}{\lang2057 p}{\lang2057 tion, putting options into a ranking order, assigning values on a given scale, or using some other choice model. The provision of a choice model to a certain extent depends on which outcomes are expected of the poll. \par \par }{\b\lang2057 Expertise}{\lang2057 : a specific group of people (or even a single person) with authority in a certain field are asked to provide a rating ofa certain object according to their specific knowledge.\par \par A main difference between polling and expertise lies  in the selection of individuals who may contribute their ratings. In polling, a certain population is addressed, while in an application of expertise only specific individuals are asked for their ratings.\par \par Polling and expertise are common ways to solicit ratings from people. In both cases ind}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 vidual ratings are usually \lquote demanded\rquote , as people are requested  to pr}{\lang2057 o}{\lang2057 vide ratings. }{\i\lang2057 Demanded}{\lang2057  ratings are given by people when they are deliberately asked to evaluate a particular option, in many cases together with other options. However, speaking of }{\i\lang2057 demanded}{\lang2057  ratings implies there are also  }{\i\lang2057 voluntary}{\lang2057  ratings.  }{\i\lang2057 Voluntary}{\lang2057  ratings are given by people every time they feel like expressing their opinion.\par \par There seems to be an obvious need for demanded ratings in everyday life, because they support decision-making. However, with voluntary ratings there is the problem that possibly only a small number of persons  may provide ratings. To increase participation, one has to think of incentives, or it has to be clear what people may gain from rating (see efforts of collaborative rating). One possibility to foster rating activities is to link them to the role organisation of a group. Appe}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 dix 1 gives an example of  such a scenario.\par \par Polling and expertise are methods to collect ratings which seem to be highly relevant for co}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 puter-mediated communication. For example, polling is employed whenever preference sche}{\lang2057 d}{\lang2057 ules of individuals are investigated in order to build information filters in the course of co}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 puter supported collaborative rating (e.g. \lquote firefly\rquote ), but also whenever people are asked to vote on a subject via computer networks. Expertise is employed whenever content on the Internet is rated by a trusted authority (e.g. \lquote PICS\rquote ).\par \par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505007}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505089}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505153}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505321}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382719221}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828870}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896589}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011200}1.4. A Procedural Definition of Rating{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505007}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505089}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505153}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505321}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382719221}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828870}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896589}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011200}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 As mentioned above, rating can be seen as an action by which some value is assigned to an o}{\lang2057 b}{\lang2057 ject. To understand the scope and impact of those actions one must also think of their conditions and consequences. Thus, individual actions may be linked to collective outcomes. Therefore a definition of a rating process is suggested which brings together its individual and collective nature.\par \par }\pard \qj\li567\widctlpar\box\brdrs\brdrw30\brsp100 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 Definition:\par }{\i\lang2057 In a rating process }{\b\i\lang2057 values}{\i\lang2057  are assigned to }{\b\i\lang2057 objects}{\i\lang2057   under certain }{\b\i\lang2057 conditions}{\i\lang2057  with certain }{\b\i\lang2057 consequences}{\i\lang2057 .\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }{\b\lang2057 Value:\par }{\lang2057 The value assigned to an object in a rating process may express a degree along  a certain dime}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 sion.\par \par There are many dimensions of rating imaginable, the most common being \lquote quality\rquote  and \lquote approval\rquote .\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 \lquote Quality\rquote : An object may be assigned a value to indicate the degree to which it has a certain quality, e.g. the handling of a car may be rated with 1 to 5 stars in a consumer test. A very common quality criterion on  the Internet is \lquote decency\rquote . In this case the object of rating is the available content. For example, Net Shepherd (1996) uses five categories - or values - (general, child, pre-teen, adult, objectionable) to restrict children's access to material on the Internet. Many cases in which an object is evaluated in terms of quality rely on a method of rating which was introduced as \lquote expertise\rquote . But \lquote polling\rquote  also occurs, especially when people's preferences are investigated like in collaborative rating.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 \lquote Approval\rquote : An object may be assigned a value to indicate the degree to which it is a}{\lang2057 p}{\lang2057 proved or agreed upon . This is most common in voting processes investigating  people's consent. The evaluation of objects in terms of approval mainly relies on the method of \lquote polling\rquote . \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par Note that in most cases where an object is apparently  just put into a certain category, it is actually assigned a value because an underlying ranking order between this category and others may be assumed (see the rating of films).\par \par \par }{\b\lang2057 Object:\par }{\lang2057 The object receiving a certain value in a rating process may be anything forming the content of some communication. \par \par Currently the most common objects of rating in computer-mediated communication are:\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 the content of webpages\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 the contributions in discussion forums (newsgroups).\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par Both on webpages and in newsgroups the object of rating is some content which is already available. It is a different matter, when an object is made the content of some communication in order to initiate a rating process. Thus topics or persons may become objects of rating (which sometimes takes the form of voting).\par \par \par }{\b\lang2057 Conditions:\par }{\lang2057 Every action of assigning values to objects is determined to a certain extent by the conditions of the rating process. \par \par These conditions, which every rating procedure must satisfy , cover  several questions:\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\sb120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls11\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls11\adjustright {\lang2057 What are the objects of rating?\par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl12\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\ilvl12\adjustright {\lang2057 The objects of rating may be presented, suggested, or free to decide. \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\sb120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls12\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls12\adjustright {\lang2057 Which value system is used?\par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl12\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\ilvl12\adjustright {\lang2057 The values may be expressed in quantitative (e.g. three hearts) or qualitative (e.g. \lquote adult\rquote ) terms. In most cases a single value system will be given, but di}{\lang2057 f}{\lang2057 ferent value systems which one is  free to choose may also be offered.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\sb120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls13\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls13\adjustright {\lang2057 Who may perform ratings?\par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl12\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\ilvl12\adjustright {\lang2057 The participation in a rating process may be limited (e.g. membership or appointment) or open to everyone.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 4.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\sb120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls14\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls14\adjustright {\lang2057 Who may see the ratings?\par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl12\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\ilvl12\adjustright {\lang2057 The audience of the outcomes of a rating process may be restricted to the people who pa}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 ticipated or may include  everyone.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\sb120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls15\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls15\adjustright {\lang2057 Is a rating demanded or is it performed fully at people\rquote s discretion?\par }\pard \qj\li284\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 This points to different scopes of the solicitation to answer. Either a rating is strongly d}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 manded to advance decision-making in a certain situation, or any rating is \lquote welcome\rquote  just to contribute to the production of meta-information. In the first case, people are asked to give a single rating at a certain time on a certain object, while in the second case people are pe}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 manently asked to rate any object, but without much insistence.\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par One may imagine many more questions if one goes into the details of specific rating procedures. In  principle, one may assume that the conditions tend to be strict if ratings are closely connected with consequences. This is most evident in the case of voting, where strong restrictions are needed to guarantee a legitimate outcome, e.g. what are the topics? (agenda setting), who are the voters? (registration, confidentiality).\par \par \par }{\b\lang2057 Consequences: \par }{\lang2057 Every action of assigning a value to some object(s) eventually leads to consequences on an ind}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 vidual and a collective level. \par \par These consequences depend on the need for results/?/. Accordingly, individual ratings may be processed differently either to serve information filtering or decision-making.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\sb120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls16\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls16\adjustright {\lang2057 Decision making:\par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl12\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\ilvl12\adjustright {\lang2057 Individual ratings are collected and aggregated according to the need to come to decisions.\par In voting procedures, the search for a \lquote winner\rquote  is done by counting algorithms.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\sb120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls17\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls17\adjustright {\lang2057 Information filtering:\par }\pard \qj\li284\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 Individual ratings are collected and aggregated according to the need of building up filters for information selection. In filtering procedures algorithms are used to predict a person\rquote s inte}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 est in specific information. Filters may be designed so as to learn from a single person\rquote s e}{\lang2057 x}{\lang2057 perience in information selection (\lquote agents\rquote ) and/or from the experience of a group of people (collaborative filtering).\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par To illustrate our conception of value, object, conditions and consequences, some examples of rating and voting in everyday life and on the Internet are given in }{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\lang2057  REF _Ref382896133 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt {\lang2057 Table 2}}}{\lang2057 .\par \par }\pard\plain \s18\sb120\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Ref382896133}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896178}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011414}Table }{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\lang2057  SEQ Table \\* ARABIC }}{\fldrslt {\lang1024 2}}}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Ref382896133}: Examples of voting and rating{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896178}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011414}\par }\trowd \trgaph70\trleft-70\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw15 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cltxlrtb \cellx1772\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cltxlrtb \cellx3119\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cltxlrtb \cellx4678\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cltxlrtb \cellx6909\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdr