cedures in terms of actions, conditions and consequences has been  suggested. This perspective views rating as an action which is to assign a value to an object under certain conditions with certain consequences. Values may refer to the degree of quality or to the degree of approval. Objects may be any content of (computer-mediated) communication. The conditions of a rating process determine the objects, the values, the participation and the audience. The cons}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 quence of a rating process may be decision-making - like in the case of voting procedures - or information filtering. This conception should help to spot similarities and differences among the many forms of rating in everyday life and in computer-mediated communication, as well as to imagine new ways of using rating activities to enrich computer-mediated communication.\par \par It is important to note that despite the above argument for an integrative view of voting and rating at the level of social action, it will be necessary to provide distinct tools for voting and for rating at the user interface level.\par \par \sect }\sectd \psz9\linex0\endnhere\sectdefaultcl {\header \pard\plain \s28\qj\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \tqr\tx9072\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\f1\fs20 Voting and Telecommunications\tab }{\field{\*\fldinst {\cs38\f1\fs20  PAGE }}{\fldrslt {\cs38\f1\fs20\lang1024 26}}}{\f1\fs20 \par }}\pard\plain \s1\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\lang1031\kerning28\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505009}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505091}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505154}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505322}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373565140}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373651948}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373660841}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828872}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896591}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011202}2. Rating in Everyday Life{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505009}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505091}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505154}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505322}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373565140}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373651948}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373660841}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828872}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896591}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011202}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par We all rate objects and events in our environment and our life. We evaluate persons, their a}{\lang2057 c}{\lang2057 tions towards us and towards others. We rate objects according to their usefulness for specific purposes and actions for their appropriateness. We all personally exchange assessments of those items, be it by gossip, by serious consultation, or by formal channels. If we try to introduce rating schemes in the electronic communication environment it could help to look at rating in traditional social environments. Our emphasis in this respect will be on formalised, institutionalised forms of rating in our social communities. Having set this goal, it becomes evident very soon that many different forms of ratings exist: grading in schools, rating of personnel in companies, assessment and evaluation of projects and programmes, rating of co}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 sumer goods by consumer associations, professional evaluation of working tools, asses}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 ment of performance in sports, awarding prizes, medals and honours in literature and the arts.\par \par What are the common features of those schemes? What can we learn from them in devising new rating mechanisms? Will rating mechanisms in the future be modelled after traditional forms, or will new forms emerge? Which ingredients should  be preserved? In what way do they fail? Which additional benefits can electronic communication offer?\par \par Studying rating procedures, we must deal with several questions:  What are the functions of rating mechanisms? What purposes does a rating fulfil? What are its uses for the recipient? How is rating performed? What are the methods of rating? Experts, panels, formal mathematical methods, experimental set-ups and other methods contribute to the establishment of ratings. Who will use the ratings? How are ratings distributed - are they for internal use by a co}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 pany, a school, an institution, are they distributed widely and are they - at least in principle - open to the public?\par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505010}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505092}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505155}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505323}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373565141}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373651949}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373660842}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828873}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896592}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011203}2.1. Functions of Rating{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505010}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505092}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505155}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505323}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373565141}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373651949}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373660842}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828873}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896592}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011203}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par We shall  start with a list of traditional forms of rating. One may  divide  ratings into three types:\par - assessment of the performance of individuals\par - reduction of uncertainty in making decisions\par - assessment of output and achievement.\par \par It is obvious that these functions are not clearly separated. Many rating mechanisms have se}{\lang2057 v}{\lang2057 eral purposes and different users of a rating will consider different functions as important. However, to achieve some order in this area it could be useful to employ the above preliminary classification. We shall leave out evaluation by the marketplace: prices are a form of rating. On the one hand they are indicators of usefulness but they are also influenced by scarcity and the effort needed to pr}{\lang2057 o}{\lang2057 duce goods  (expressed by capital and labour costs). However, it is self-evident that many of the rating schemes listed below are somehow - directly or indirectly - related to economic pe}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 formance. We should  be aware that the different forms of rating have quite different social and political influence: ratings by big credit institutions may determine the future fate of companies and their employees. They may even topple governments if a nation loses  its creditwo}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 thiness (Martin, Schumann 1996, p.99). We will study those features in turn and remark on their implications for CMC.\par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505011}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505093}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011204}Assessment of the Performance of Individuals{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505011}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505093}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011204}\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505012}Grading in school and higher education{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505012}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 We will start with this kind of rating as most of us experienced it  as the first formal rating scheme we became acquainted with. It is perceived as a grading of the knowledge and ability of the pupil or student. Empirical work (Kalthoff 1996, Stiggins and Conkin 1992, Brookhart 1993) shows the complex setting of grading practices. Teachers adapt their grading to the average performance of the class. They orient themselves after  the best pupils and restru}{\lang2057 c}{\lang2057 ture their assessment according to averages (arithmetic means, number of pupils failing a given task). Teachers perceive the results of examinations as a test of their own performance. Have they been successful in explaining concepts and teaching skills? This becomes more explicit in objective forms of examination, which is most often the case in final exams. Those are supe}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 vised by external experts (in most cases teachers themselves) and in some countries the tasks are set  externally. Comparison of grades by the instructor with those by independent (outside) experts provides clues on the ability and reliability of the instructor. We learn from this that the responsibility of those who have to rate depends on their role in the production - evaluation/rating - consequences cycle.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505013}Personnel evaluation{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505013}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Something similar is true for personnel evaluation. Assessment of personnel to be hired and on the job can often be seen as much as an evaluation of the evaluator as one of the person to be evaluated. The literature on personnel management provides a number of different models which are impl}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 mented in differentbranches of industry. Performance rating on the job is another name for the same task. The publications of the American Management Association may serve as an example (Grote 1996).\par \par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Psychological testing\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Well known and also used in personnel evaluation are the different versions of intelligence tests. Introduced originally to measure the intelligence of mentally handicapped persons they were wid}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 ly  used in the seventies and eighties. Although enthusiasm for this kind of testing has somewhat diminished, it is still very common. Following this, psychologists and to some extent sociologists devised a large number of experiments to measure psychological parameters of persons, and to rank them along scales e}{\lang2057 x}{\lang2057 pressing various properties.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505014}Evaluation of crimes{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505014}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The evaluation of crimes is a typical multi-stage process: first one body, the legislature, form}{\lang2057 u}{\lang2057 lates guidelines on the length of containment in jail or the fines for conceivable crimes or o}{\lang2057 f}{\lang2057 fences, or an existing collection of cases, textbooks and precedents provides this framework. Then, in a given case, another body of persons - the judge, a jury - has to determine the specific penalty taking into account the special circumstances of the offence. Even the mitigating and aggravating circumstances are legally codified, there are precedents and a body of literature on them (e.g. Pallin 1982).\par \par To some extent the legal system codifies the moral convictions of a society and provides the means of  enforcing them. The purposes of punishment vary: prevention of future crimes and compensation or punishment for misbehaviour are the major factors contributing to the justification of these most elaborate rating systems of our societies. The text by Pallin quoted above describes these factors in more detail. A very specific detail in this form of rating is the elaborate justification which goes with every verdict. If voters had to express their decisions always - or at least more often - in terms of well established principles, consensus would  be more easily achievable.\par \par Even if one does not (yet) institutionalise the legal system in the electronic environment, it provides an example for the rating of undesired events and results. The rating of content on WWW and the Internet as pornographic or politically incorrect may provide a first meeting point of net technologies and the legal system. The multinational nature of the networks will make progress in this area dependent on long-term international consultation and neg}{\lang2057 o}{\lang2057 tiation. This does not prevent individual rating agencies from providing voluntary tools to discourage u}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 wanted content.\par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505015}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505094}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011205}Reduction of Uncertainty in Decision-Making{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505015}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505094}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011205}\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505016}Product and service rating (consumer associations, special interest and technical journals){\*\bkmkend _Toc373505016}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The increased diversification of goods and services makes it more and more difficult to choose products according to functionality and quality. Internationalisation of supply and regionally diversified marketing strategies make the orientation of consumers, but also of professional users of investment goods and services ever more complicated. This has led to the emergence of co}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 sumer associations and professional consulting services.\par These consumer associations, professional consulting companies, technical journals and special interest magazines regularly publish reviews and comparative assessments of consumer pro}{\lang2057 d}{\lang2057 ucts and professional working tools. The outcome ranges from vague recommendations to tables listing many different features of the items compared. Different features are often combined (most often by weighted sums) to achieve an overall assessment. This is done by (hopefully) independent institutions and journals.\par The producers or providers of the respective goods or services use those results intensively if they are favourable. In regions where it is permitted they set up similar tables themselves - u}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 ing only those features which are favourable to their product. The declared aim of independent rating institutions is the unbiased information of users and consumers. However, consumer associations report (Spitalsky 198x) that the producers react to the results of the reviews and change their product designs and features.\par \par This area of direct influence on sales therefore often comes under pressure by the rated organisations. That means  objective tests and assessments of this kind need some proof of their validity and trustworthiness. Trust in organisational arrangements or in i}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 dividuals performing such comparisons is built up only slowly. In the end, the consumer associations  must provide consistently useful ratings to be accepted by the public or the professionals concerned.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505017}Credit rating of individuals, companies and political institutions{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505017}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Banks and suppliers of goods and services have a need to assess the creditworthiness of cu}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 tomers with whom they do business. Suppliers often use information by banks and f}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 nancial institutions to deal with unknown companies. Specialised service companies collect and compile information of this kind. The procedures range from elaborate assessment of the economic performance and standing of companies or countries to over\-\_the \_counter lending supported by simple questionnaires and  using computers to assess the creditworthiness of small-scale debtors.\par \par As an example, the table below presents  rating definitions used by large international rating institutions which evaluate (inter alia) the banks themselves (a3eco 1996):\par \par }\pard \qj\sa120\keep\keepn\wi