governments) and companies (firms). Similar but often simpler rating schemes are applied in credit rating to companies in the national environment. Credit rating is directly related to the interest rates charged. The rating of countries by Moody's may increase interest by 3.8 percent between Aaa (Triple A) and B (Martin, Schumann 1994, p.98).\par \par The process of credit rating by banks is quite elaborate. All kinds of data about the individual or the company are assembled and complex calculations based on statistical reasoning and the latest developments in mathematical decision-making under uncertainty are used. Banks the}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 selves use computer networks to refer complicated and risky decisions to central authorisation units. In this case the central unit and the manager in the outpost who knows the local circu}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 stances can have the same information on the screen and often decide on the rating together.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505018}Evaluation of project proposals{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505018}\par }\pard\plain \s5\qj\sb240\sa60\widctlpar\outlinelevel4\adjustright \f1\fs22\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The evaluation of projects is the classical area of evaluation. Guba and Lincoln (1993) describe four different steps in the development of evaluations (their main field are  educational projects on which their theoretical evaluation ideas draw heavily):\par Measurement (psychological/ educational tests): the evaluator as technician.\par Description: characteristics of achievement and non-achievement. Strength and weaknesses in the attainment of specified objectives.\par Judgement: drawing conclusions about the evaluand's  success, effectiveness or utility.\par Constructive negotiation (see below in the methods section).\par At this point we may state that for the phase of project formulation and proposal evaluation communication is of high importance. Not all discussions can be substituted by CMC but it helps in the follow-up of personal discussions and presentations. Reformulation in the proposal situation is well suited for negotiations with - and involvement of - special interestgroups. Clear cut ratings are possible but have to take into account sensitive issues.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505019}Evaluation of medical interventions{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505019}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The assessment of medical treatment is a sensitive issue. The traditional double blind exper}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 ments try to avoid some common pitfalls of the introduction of bias by the experimenter. While a ce}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 tain freedom in interpretation remains, the procedures are mainly based on measurement and experiment, which is the first method of evaluation in well-defined circumstances.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505022}Agenda-setting in the political arena{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505022}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Agenda setting is a political process influenced by pressure groups, social partners and subliminal i}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 fluences of different kinds. It is the area of what we call the  negotiation environment of programme assessment and evaluation. New electronic media may contribute to the public discussion aspect of agenda setting. \par \par An example how ratings can be used in this area is provided by VoteSmart, an institution which tries to deliver impartial information for USA voters. One of its approaches is to show ratings of US Congress members by different special interest groups. The idea (independent of the Web or Internet) is to count the number of cases where the member voted according to the interest of a given interest group. This kind of performance evaluation is an esta}{\lang2057 b}{\lang2057 lished practice among those groups.\par \par VoteSmart collects evaluations of this kind by many different interest groups and describes the results thus:\par \par }\pard \qj\li1134\ri1134\widctlpar\adjustright {\fs20\lang2057 'These evaluations are in percentage form. They represent the percentage of time that the i}{\fs20\lang2057 n}{\fs20\lang2057 cumbent voted with that organisation's preferred positions on a number of votes that they identified as key in their issue area. Remember, by definition, these ratings by special interest groups are biased. They do not represent a non-partisan stance. In addition, some groups select votes that tend to favour members of one political party over another, rather than selecting votes based solely on issue concerns. However, they can be invaluable in showing where an incumbent has stood on a series of votes over a year's time, especially when ratings by groups on all sides of an issue are compared. Descriptions of the organisations offering performance evaluations are available.'\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par The effort shows how even biased ratings may be used to get a comparatively clear overall picture of an area filled with subjective and interest-loaded judgements. The message to us could be: always watch who is rating. Ratings themselves give clues about the rating unit (be it a person or an institution).\par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505023}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505095}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011206}Assessment of Output and Achievement{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505023}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505095}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011206}\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505024}Measurement of performance{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505024}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 In some disciplines in sport and in competitive games easily definable procedures help to measure performance and to rate achievement. Not too many of those achievements are related to activ}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 ties in the digital environment. It is, however, no conceptual problem to show results on a new medium and to consider new forms of competition taking place directly on the networks.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505025}Evaluation of projects{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505025}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 This refers to the evaluation of projects both during and after their implementation. Comparative evaluation of completed projects are not popular in the political arena but can give valuable insights for  the planning of further activities.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505026}Literary criticism - selection by publishers - peer review{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505026}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Judgement of texts by publishers, readers and by literary critics determines the success of texts. This is true for both success in the market place and also for recognition in the more elevated circles of elitist literature.\par A somewhat more formalised and theoretically more impartial solution is the peer review pro}{\lang2057 c}{\lang2057 ess which tries to guarantee a fair selection process. It is still the best method we have, a}{\lang2057 l}{\lang2057 though it is sometimes distorted by influential individuals or unscrupulous groups of insiders. A recent article in the Scientific American describes how difficult it is for scientists from third world countries to publish in journals quoted in the Science Citation Index, and how difficult it is for third world journals to be accepted in the Index. (This Index, however, obviously works in the opposite way  to the former Vatican Index Librorum Prohibitorum , which worked by exclusion rather than inclusion).\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505027}Honours, medals and prizes (literature, film, arts, sciences){\*\bkmkend _Toc373505027}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Ratings are frequently employed when prizes  are awar}{\lang2057 d}{\lang2057 ed in the  arts and sciences . These awards may range from fairly local events with limited appeal to outsiders to those of  world-wide reputation like the Oscar awarded by the American Film Academy, or  the Nobel Prize. \par \par These two events also show the main proponents of such events which keep them alive: institutions wishing to promote a cause, e.g. an industry (in our case the film indu}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 try), the  public interest (if the sponsor is a national or local government), or individuals with a special interest in some area - Alfred Nobel is by far not the only example. The Nobel Prize became famous because of the money involved. However, that does not mean this prize  would not remain famous if the money were suddenly no longer available. Some of the prizes  are voted on by large groups of people and therefore represent a certain consensus within the respective group.\par \par A prize is not necessarily only directed at professed purposes. Often, more or less veiled purposes - of political, manipulative or even tax evasive character - may also be present. Prestigious prizes definitely enhance the recipients' reputation and are, directly or indirectly, also sources of economic benefit.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505028}Achieving specific goals: social, technical, environmental{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505028}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Professional societies recognise the achievement of some of those goals by conferring awards or prizes. Other achievements are honoured by the government through tax benefits, grants or contracts Honours and prizes are a form of recognition. Some organisations or publications, however, also list institutions and persons independently of achievement. They praise those active for a cause, or behaving in the right way, while other institutions expose  the worst offenders against  expected b}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 haviour.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505029}Peace and human understanding{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505029}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 We take this as an example of widely recognised contributions which are still difficult to define. Receiving a peace prize does not prevent recipients from going to war later on. The prize may also serve as  an incentive  to abstain from further horrors. One should not underestimate this: such a prize, too, is an achiev}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 ment! Increasing awareness of refugees, starvation, the dangers of war and the mech}{\lang2057 a}{\lang2057 nisms leading to them is worthwhile pursuing, even if certain peace efforts  sometimes go astray. \par \par On the opposite end there are medals and praise  for acts of war, war heroes are presented as outstanding examples. Rating as an abstract concept is neutral. It is as individuals or as institutions that we must take our stand.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505030}Overall achievement, a place in history{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505030}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Long-term rating of achievement is expressed by recognition in the textbooks on a certain  field or subject , or by  inclusion in the general book of history. While even here some mechanisms are at work to remember and honour your own kin (those winning the wars write history) generally a certain detac}{\lang2057 h}{\lang2057 ment allows a more sober view. The sheer need to concentrate on the essential contributes to fairer rating. History writing may bring to the surface contributions that were not recognised during the lifetime of the originator(s) as foreru}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 ners of important developments. But history rarely rectifies misjudgement during the lifetime of able contributors and in a very precise sense it comes too late for them anyhow.\par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505031}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505096}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505156}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505324}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373565142}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373651950}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373660843}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828874}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896593}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011207}2.2. Methods of Rating{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505031}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505096}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505156}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505324}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373565142}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373651950}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373660843}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828874}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896593}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011207}\par }\pard\plain \s19\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\lang2057\cgrid {\par The methods of rating are influenced by a number of factors:\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s19 \f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \s19\qj\fi-283\li283\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {Rational measurement and assessment of performance\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s19 \f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \s19\qj\fi-283\li283\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {Using experiments and related observations, measurements, mathematical and statistical models to simulate or replicate behaviour\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s19 \f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \s19\qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {Discussing arguments in the context of the knowledge to which the rated object or person contributes\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s19 \f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \s19\qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {Tactical and strategic considerations are included in the rating to achieve a political, personal, or group-oriented objective. Sometimes the real purpose is somewhat hidden and the rating assumes a manipulative character.\par }\pard \s19\qj\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505032}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505097}\par According to our \lquote basic conception  of rating and voting\rquote  (see section 1) the following e}{x}{amples may be related to the two general methods of achieving ratings: polling and expertise.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011208}Measurement and experiment }{\b0\i\lang2057 (polling, expertise)}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Toc373505032}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505097}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011208}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 We have already spoken about measurement in sports. Testing of products is another area where many features or ingredients of rating may be determined by the experimental conditions. Those may also include users giving their subjective opinion on some quality. How and under which circumstances this is done determines whether the whole procedure still is an act of measurement. The problem then is rather one of  how to aggregate the different results in an overall rating for a specific purpose. As long as the individual (partial) test results are made accessible, alternative ratings for special purposes can be derived. An appropriate digital environment is well suited to support this re\_evaluation.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505033}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505098}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011209}Specialists, opinion leaders }{\b0\i\lang2057 (expertise)}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Toc373505033}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505098}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011209}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Many, if not most, formal ratings are done by experts and specialists. They try to establish fo}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 mal procedures to reach an objective result. These may be experimental set-ups in which co}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 sumer products or other items are measured.\par \par Thus, the International Organisation of Consumer Unions, IOCU, publishes guidelines for testing. The European Testing Group (ETG) organises co-operative testing of products by different national testing organisations. Additionally to the staff of those institutions, experts are enlisted for the special area of the product under consideration. The properties of the product under scrutiny are functional quality, durability, safety and security, ease of handling and price. The properties praised  in advertisements, legal r}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 quirements, standards, environmental compatibility, service, warranty, and the availability of spare parts, packing, ease of installation, and manuals all have to be taken into account. Experiments show that independent evaluation of products by consumers highly correlates with test results. A certain problem is posed by overall assessment. This global rating assumes an average consumer who may not coincide with the individual need of specific consumers. This di}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 crepancy is accepted to achieve high visibility and clarity and to reach consumers with widely varying educational backgrounds.\par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505034}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505099}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011210}Juries and panels{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505034}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505099}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011210}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Prizes, medals and honours (and court sentences) are often conferred by juries. In most of the cases the jury is a group of experts in a given field. Consider the procedure for awarding the Nobel Prize: Each year the respective committees send individual invitations to thousands of scientists, members of academies and university professors in numerous countries, asking them to nom}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 nate Nobel Prize candidates for the coming year. Those considered competent to submit nominations are chosen in such a way that as many countries and universities as possible are represented. These prize nominations must reach the respective committees before February 1 of the year for which the nomination is made. \par }\pard\plain \s15\qj\li567\ri1134\sb120\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \ldblquote The nominations received by each committee are then investigated with the help of specially appointed experts. When the committees have made their selection among the nominated candidates and have pr}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 sented their recommendations to the prize-awarding institutions, a vote is taken for the final choice of laureates. Prize decisions are announced immediately after the vote in October each year. Competence to nominate candidates for the Nobel Prizes varies somewhat among the prize-awarding institutions ... \rdblquote .\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par Juries sometimes work in very informal ways but some also have highly structured procedures. One may think that the selection of the jury could be more important than the procedure, but in some cases well-defined, published procedures are most important to win  acceptance of the rating by the users.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505035}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505100}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011211}Peer groups }{\b0\i\lang2057 (expertise)}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Toc373505035}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505100}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011211}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The paradigm of assessment in science is peer review. What will be published in influential journals is determined by anonymised scientific peers. The editors of the respective journals send the articles in question to peer scientists competent to judge the content. Those do or do not recommend  public}{\lang2057 a}{\lang2057 tion. They often provide further guidance and helpful comments on the paper and make publ}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 cation dependent on meeting certain conditions. Due to the widespread use of Internet publishing in some fields of science (50% of all physics papers are pre-published on the e-print-Archive in Los Alamos, New Mexico), the discussion on ele}{\lang2057 c}{\lang2057 tronic alternatives to peer review has advanced furthest. A whole OECD conference in June 1996 was dedicated to "The Global Research Village" (The Economist, 1996).\par \par One solution would be to add comments to every paper so that  subsequent readers would see them. Another one would be to attribute stars to the paper, like in the Michelin Guide (one of the consumer product rating schemes). The chief worry is "that high\_quality work will be drowned in a flood of dub}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 ous data and poor prose." However, peer review also lets a lot of this pass. Normally the author of a paper gives up his copyright to the scientific journal. Publishing on the Internet means formally keeping the copyright, but giving it away free of charge. Should the government interfere? Similarly to the way suggested by the Danish minister of research and information technology who pr}{\lang2057 o}{\lang2057 posed  to provide high\_speed communication links if all  scientists published their results on the Internet! Clearly a final model to replace peer review has not yet been found. Web4Groups is a place to experiment on this.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505036}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505101}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011212}Social discussion, bargaining processes and negotiations of those concerned }{\b0\i\lang2057 (expertise)}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Toc373505036}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505101}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011212}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Evaluation of proposals, or of ongoing or completed projects, isoften performed by a 'political' negotiation process of the parties concerned. The proponents of 4th Generation Evaluation (Guba and Lincoln 1989) argue that evaluation in such circumstances should prefer this  way of 'constructing' a joint view of the envisaged changes and of its effects on groups with diffe}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 ing interests. Such a process will often not be satisfied with ranking a limited number of pr}{\lang2057 o}{\lang2057 posals but will actively involve interest groups in developing proposals.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505037}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505102}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011213}Opinion polls }{\b0\i\lang2057 (polling)}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Toc373505037}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505102}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011213}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Opinion polls are often conducted to find out the preferences of the public or of  a specific target group. Normally, a limited set of options is proposed and preferences are looked for. \par The main problem on the Internet so far is representativity. Only target groups with a strong commitment to the net will be appropriate  for this method.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505038}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505103}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011214}Automatic procedures{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505038}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505103} }{\b0\i\lang2057 (polling)}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Toc384011214}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 In electronic environments many procedures may be automated: users or visitors of a Website or another entry point to a computer may be observed. Inferences about their preferences can be drawn automatically by analysing their path through the computer, the Web site, or the Web at large. These ratings can then be combined in an adequate way and be presented to the user himself (perhaps for correction or to get a more explicit rating from him), to the managers of the site, or (at least in aggregate form) to the other visitors of the site ("our most successful pages are", "this URL lured 20.000 repeating visitors", or more complicated inferences). There is some concern about the transparent customer about whom the shopping centre knows more than he does of himself. Should one encourage this? Should one return some of the information to the user/customer who provided it in the first place? Where are the limits?\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505039}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505104}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011215}Stratified or general rating }{\b0\i\lang2057 (polling)}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Toc373505039}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505104}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011215}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Rating may even be done by individuals. However, in many cases the rating should represent a totality of a certain sort. In this case one has the choice of either everybody contributing to the rating process of all items or issues, or  that the group elects representatives to do the actual assessment for them (this can also be a single person). A}{\lang2057 l}{\lang2057 ternatively, a random sample from the totality can perform this rating.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505040}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505105}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011216}Stratified samples, guided opinion formation }{\b0\i\lang2057 (polling)}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Toc373505040}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505105}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011216}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 A variant of the random sample is the approach that one  assembles a random sample of the totality selected by main characteristics of the population (stratified sample), and to perform guided opinion formation with it. Assuming that the average person selected will not be too competent in the chosen area, experts will present the main positions and issues in the area for which a rating is due. After pre}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 entation and guidance, the group is left alone to come to a common conclusion in a negotiation phase.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505041}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505106}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011217}Contributions of voluntary donors }{\b0\i\lang2057 (polling)}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Toc373505041}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505106}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011217}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Simple rating tools on the WWW give every visitor the opportunity to leave his preferences on a limited number of topics or items. Those are assembled in overall ratings or statistical distributions of results.\par \par This resembles street activists who collect opinions on debated issues by asking passers-by to answer questions or fill in forms. The method has some justification if the visited arena is rather specia}{\lang2057 l}{\lang2057 ised and its visitors are reasonably well behaved. It may also be suited for making some public outcry or the complete negligence of an item more generally known. \par \par If the aim is a prediction,  based on questions put  to a sample of the whole population, of the choices which would have been made if every single member of a large population had been asked, then statistics requires stringent ways of selecting the sample, using random sampling, stratified sampling, etc. However, in many everyday processes, there is no such goal to predict the opinion of a larger population. Quite the opposite, it is often valuable to obtain ratings made by individuals who have time to careful  evaluation before deciding on their ratings. In this case it may be desirable to identify the persons giving the ratings and to make them recognisable.\par \par The fact that statistical predictions require stringent sampling methods does not imply that all kinds of ratings done by only a selected number of people must to be regarded as unacceptable. In any case, through  these methods one will gather ideas about what may be controversial or where there is little dissent.\par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828875}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896594}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011218}2.3. Rating and Measurement (a comment by Dieter Bichlbauer){\*\bkmkend _Toc382828875}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896594}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011218}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par Complex rating, like testing of goods or student reports, is often quite similar to measurement. But there is one important difference between complex rating and physical measurement: rating always entails interaction between the judges, the objects of rating (if the objects are persons) and the recipients. These interactions consist of mutual expectations (e.g. between teacher and students), or anticipated expectations (e.g. the expectations of consumers anticipated by the tester of goods) in the case of indirect interactions. The interactive character of the rating context may have an important influence on rating which will be discussed later.\par \par Complex rating requires the selection of dimensions with which the object of rating can be d}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 scribed. These dimensions must be relevant to the purpose of rating. The selection of the d}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 mensions is not only a technical problem. It must be adequate for the wishes of the recipients too. The rating of cars should not be restricted to technical dimensions, it should also include "human dimensions" such as comfort, handling, etc.. The dimensions should create a nearly complete picture of the object fitting  the wide range of possible purposes of the recip}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 ents.\par \par Indicators are the link between dimensions and objects. The indicator shows the location of a concrete object on the dimension. It is easy to find indicators for the security of cars, because security standards exist. But which indicators are adequate for the dimension of handling?\par \par The relations among dimension, indicator and object are quite similar to measurement. In sc}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 ence, the dimensions are defined in the respective theory, which can be compared to the picture formed by the dimensions. The indicators are deduced from these definitions (operationalisation). The indicator serves to fix the location of an object on a dimension by observation. \par \par Both measurement and rating share the same problem: there does not exist any objective method of determining the indicators. The indicators are not yielded by logical deduction, but by experience, standards, general agreements, etc.\par \par The indicators should fulfil at least three criteria of measurement:\par  (a) Internal validity: the indicator should measure the dimension from which it is deduced.\par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057  (b) Selectivity: the indicator should enable the tester to discriminate among the objects on the dimension.\par  (c) Precision (or reliability): repeated measurement of the same objects with the same ind}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 cator should lead to the same results or to results that are not significantly different.\par  (d) Objectivity: repeated measurement of the same objects with the same indicator but by different judges should yield results which are not significantly different.\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par These four criteria are also relevant for rating. However, the \lquote qualitative dimensions\rquote  entail pro}{\lang2057 b}{\lang2057 lems. There are indicators for these dimensions, too, but the locations of objects on the dimension are not really observable. So the criterion of selectivity is \lquote fulfilled\rquote  more by subje}{\lang2057 c}{\lang2057 tive decisions than by inter-subjective repeatable observations. This is also true for prec}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 sion. This problem is usually solved by expert ratings. Mostly there is high correlation among experts' ratings . Therefore the criterion of objectivity seems to be fulfilled. \par \par A rating of products may be criticised as not being precise enough, but in general ratings are judged in respect to their inter-subjective intelligibility. In the ideal case the recipient should say: \ldblquote Well, I would have done it in the same way.\rdblquote \par \par Many scientific studies have offered evidence that these criteria are not fulfilled in the case of school tests (Kalthoff 1996,107). The probability of measurement errors will be much higher for rating in interactive contexts.\par \par Here are some examples of possible errors: The validity will be violated if the teacher or a an expert of a personnel department fails to rate not only the achievement but, perhaps unco}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 sciously, also some characteristics (indicators) of the social dimension, too. The reliability requirement will not be fulfilled if a teacher changes his criteria during the rating. He begins with an idea of perfect performance in the test, but he realises later that even the good students cannot a}{\lang2057 p}{\lang2057 proximate this result. Consequently, he changes his criteria. So his datum is no longer the ideal but the results of the best students. This adjustment is necessary because too many negative marks diminish the prestige of the teacher or force him to repeat the test. This instructive e}{\lang2057 x}{\lang2057 ample also shows how rating depends on the norms of the judge's institutional environment . \par \par Complex rating procedures can proceed in two steps:  first, the rating is carried out in respect to each dimension, second, in overall dimensions. Numbers (school marks are frequently used) are attached to the objects in both steps. In measurement theory, the first step is called fundamental measurement, the second one is called derived measurement. The derived measure is yielded by combining the results (measures) of the fundamental measurements with a formula (e.g. density = mass/volume). The formulae are not arbitrary because they are based on on physical laws.\par \par The structure of the rating procedure is similar to measurement but it involves  more problems, especially in the second step. Often it is necessary to attach weights to the dimensions. One may assume that "quantitative dimensions" which can be measured much more precisely will be attributed  more importance than "qualitative" dimensions. There may be two reasons for this:\par \par (a) There exist explicit or tacit rules which cause the judge to prefer these dimensions.\par }\pard \qj\fi-426\li426\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057  (b) The rating is found to be objective in accordance with  these dimensions, consequently serious criticism can be avoided.\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505043}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505108}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505157}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505325}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373565143}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373651951}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373660844}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828876}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896595}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011219}2.4. Distribution and Dissemination of Ratings{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505043}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505108}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505157}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505325}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373565143}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373651951}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373660844}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828876}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896595}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011219}\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505044}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505109}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011220}Confidentiality - distribution policies{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505044}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505109}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011220}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The distribution or dissemination of the results of a rating process varies widely. Examination grades, personnel ratings, credit ratings are usually not distributed widely. Even if they are a}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 nounced their circulation is fairly limited. Privacy and data protection laws even forbid the pu}{\lang2057 b}{\lang2057 lication of much of this information. Research results, sports events, tests by consumer unions, as well as  prizes and honours are intended for publ}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 cation and the amount of publicity depends more on the rating unit's influence on the media than on the need for confidentiality. Many ratings within government institutions are confidential, at least as long as the final dec}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 sion has  not been announced.\par \par Every distribution mechanism will have to take into account the different needs of diverse ra}{\lang2057 t}{\lang2057 ing institutions. Some confidentiality, especially in the phase of collecting contr}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 butions to the rating process, will be necessary for many organisations. On the Internet s}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 curity features which are just beginning to stabilise will be necessary to provide such data protection.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505045}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505110}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011221}Who receives ratings{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505045}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505110}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011221}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Different phases of the rating process will involve  changing needs for the protection of inform}{\lang2057 a}{\lang2057 tion. Some prizes are awarded quite  openly: discussions on the candidates' merits may even be transmitted via TV. Other large organisations (think of the Oscar) keep intermediate results fairly concealed. Many ratings need  to be accessible to a few select persons only. In a distance education environment one may require grades to be  visible only to the examiner, the school administration and the student. In many of those situations  the rating of the rater may depend on his successful ratings.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505046}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505111}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011222}Who uses ratings{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505046}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505111}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011222}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The different functions determine the target groups of the ratings. When rating students, personnel, or  crimes, the target groups are obvious. Only serious  crimes are publicised widely, but  legal proceedings are usually public. At least in Europe it is not usual to put criminals on display as part of the punishment. However, both the police system and potential employers are interested in this kind of  information.\par \par All ratings directed to decision makers are evidently used to help making those decisions. The most diverse motivations exist for the assessment of output and achievement. Part of its  raison d'\u234\'90tre lies in establishing  and enhancing the reputation of  those whose achievements seem worthwhile for one group or another. This implies that the ideas, the results, or just the biogr}{\lang2057 a}{\lang2057 phy of the rated person, or the history of the organisation or project, become better known. In this case  it is not decisions that are in the forefront, but more long-term orientation. Those providing the ratings hope to advance a cause, to share enjoyment about achievements or to contribute to the structuring of our life. Decisions are influenced, but those conferring the award have no specific idea about the individual decisions made.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505047}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505112}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011223}Projections{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505047}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505112}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011223}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The comparison of ratings by several or many subjects may be used to find common interests among persons. Having identified a common interest (e.g. in music, science, or literature) it is possible to propose interesting items which one of them has seen and rated.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505048}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505113}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011224}How long are ratings significant{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505048}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505113}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011224}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Different forms of rating have different lifetimes. Normally, ratings are superseded by more r}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 cent ratings of the same kind. But even if their validity remains intact, their impact may change over time. Consumer product ratings would often be valid several years. Analyses of the i}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 pact of particularly good ratings for products are felt by the sales departments of the respective companies for  three to  seven  months (Spitalsky 198x).\par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505049}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505114}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505158}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505326}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373565144}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373651952}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373660845}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828877}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896596}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011225}2.5. {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505050}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505115}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505049}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505114}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505158}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505326}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373565144}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373651952}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373660845}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828877}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896596}Conclusions{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011225}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par Learning from the examination of various instances of rating in everyday life, some aspects must  be considered when thinking about the support of rating activities through computer-mediated communication.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011226}Role distribution (who is rating?){\*\bkmkend _Toc373505050}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505115}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011226}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Access control which is present in most CMC tools may be used to restrict access to the rating process. The outcome of any rating process crucially depends on who is performing or contributing to the rating. Interest groups usually have a formulated purpose and rate according to their particular interest. No}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 mally they also have an internal structure which will  define appropriate roles and procedures. \par \par If we want to implement a rating mechanism which every participant in CMC can start, we will have to define a minimum set of roles and a minimum procedure. If we are more ambitious we can devise a whole class of procedures, each with its own role set. In any case, we may assume that anybody devising a rating scheme will make the necessary preparations. Either using the pre-defined (minimum?) roles and procedures, or customising the server for specific needs. Computer support  particularly suggests itself in the aggregation process of individual contributions.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505051}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505116}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011227}Agenda setting (what is rated?){\*\bkmkend _Toc373505051}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505116}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011227}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Professional rating institutions have well-defined tasks and established ways of  selecting items to be rated. The ease of communication could make it simpler to handle larger volumes of data. An opposing  force is the need or hope for a certain quality of rating. Otherwise, the rating scheme will not be accepted or used. The alternative could be the manipulation of results by interested parties. One should be aware that already the decision to rate something is important. Even a film receiving  the predicate 'worst film ever produced' derives  some additional publicity and some fame from this. Even more important is agenda setting in the media. Creating awareness is a side-effect of  nearly any rating procedure.\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505053}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505118}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011228}Information filtering (what fits individual interests?){\*\bkmkend _Toc373505053}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505118}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011228}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 A basic problem on the Internet is the mass of information it contains. To find items of high quality fitting exactly my interests and my pressing need for certain  items of information becomes more difficult in the present rapidly  expanding  Internet. We have already described the possibility of  using joint interests to make predictions about items which could be interesting for oneself . Simpler methods just filter out unwanted information or direct the user to the areas visited before.\par \par The theoretical problem is how to classify the available information, not according to  the needs of an average user but according to those of oneself. If I believe  my interests and knowledge are represented by some classif}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 cation, I proceed to  look for a classification of material on the Internet approaching the classification I am acquainted with. Naturally, I do not need information I already have, but something which is close  enough to be useful and still complementary. To achieve this aut}{\lang2057 o}{\lang2057 matically is a topic of research in statistics and artificial intelligence, but practical results are still far away.\par On the other hand, it continues to be likely that the most important way of  finding out about relevant new developments is through personal contact with other persons of  similar - but not too similar - ideas, interests and background.\par \par Additional labels or ratings by special interest groups could help to narrow down the field where to search for specific information. The traditional keepers of information have been the librarians. They have a wealth of experience in classifying information for users. They have been elaborating  their  traditional tools, i.e. catalogues and card indexes, but many of their ideas go  beyond that. One should transfer several of the librarians' virtues to the cybersphere (see appendix 3 for first applications of rating on the Internet). \par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Decision-making (what are the collective preferences?)\par }\pard\plain \s19\qj\nowidctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\lang2057\cgrid {There are some differences between face-to-face meetings with (synchronous types of) voting and computer-supported voting. Computer support allows the handling of much larger nu}{m}{bers of participants. More complex sequences of questions may be designed to give answers to several aspects of the problem under discussion. Several and more elaborate aggregation rules may be used and discussed. Computer support also allows continuous voting, where every participant may see the result of the votes up to that point  and may change his or her vote accordingly. This allows decision procedures similar  to Delphi studies in which experts adapt their estimates of future events and of ratings of options under the influence of arguments held by other panelists. Often but not always a better consensus may be achieved.\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par }\pard\plain \s1\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\lang1031\kerning28\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828878}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896597}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011230}3. Voting and Telecommunications{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828878}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896597}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011230}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par At first sight, voting in the context of telecommunications points to the question of \lquote tele-democracy\rquote . This term was  chosen for  various efforts to improve the efficiency of democratic systems in industrialised countries through the use of new media.}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ For a review of literature and research on \lquote teledemocracy\rquote  see Dutton 1992, but note that since then there has been a revival of such efforts in the course of a general Internet euphoria.\par Dutton shows that (at least until 1992) the relevant literature mainly focuses on specific examples of teledemo}{c}{racy and surprisingly often consists of reviews and syntheses. Furthermore, articles on teledemocracy can hardly be found in mainstream journals of political science but more often in popular magazines and economic jou}{r}{nals. However, an emerging interest of political scientists in \lquote science and technology studies\rquote  can be seen and teledemocracy does not have the image of a futurist project anymore.}}}{\lang2057  But voting in the context of computer-mediated communication - which is just another term to describe telecommunications on the basis of computers - should be investigated in a further r}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 spect: support of decision-making in groups smaller than the whole population of a city or country.\par \par This section presents various types of efforts which may be located in the context of tele-democracy. Because of this study's focus on feasibility and implementation issues, the question of tele-democracy is }{\i\lang2057 not}{\lang2057  discussed from the perspective of political theory. This is also due to the fact that implementation efforts in the Web4Groups project are currently co}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 centrating on group decision-making (although it should basically be provided in the Web4Groups service to extend the voting facilities to a larger scale).\par In the following  section the application of new media in group decision-making is introduced to add the  perspective of voting in small groups. The  concluding passage on the mechanics of voting discusses important findings of social choice theory, which are both relevant to \lquote large\_scale\rquote  voting (tele-voting) and \lquote small\_scale\rquote  voting (group decision-making).\par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828879}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896598}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011231}3.1. Tele-Democracy and Tele-Voting{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828879}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896598}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011231}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Efforts to establish tele-democracy are based on the assumption that the workings of polit}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 cal institutions may change due to the influence of technological development on the stru}{\lang2057 c}{\lang2057 ture of communications in a society. Learning from the  lively debate since the advent of \lquote interactive\rquote  mass media (see cable TV) one may distinguish two aspects of tele-democracy: discussion and voting. Telecommunications media may be used to make it easier for people to participate in di}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 cussions of topics that are relevant to them, or they may be used to enable people to vote on candidates or questions from distant locations. Arterton (1987) calls this a different \lquote nature of participation\rquote : dialogue versus plebiscite. In an evaluation of tele-democracy projects he makes a further distinction concerning  the \lquote focus of participation\rquote : political officials versus private persons.\par \par Thus one may divide tele-democracy projects into four types: (1) private, non-governmental plebiscites, (2) governmental dialogues, (3) governmental plebiscites and (4.) private, non-governmental dialogues. From the evaluation of various projects belonging to the different types,  Arterton concludes that plebiscitarian projects suffer from serious inherent problems (e.g. agenda setting) and that they are so far imaginable only on an experimental basis. In contrast, he believes  that \lquote dialogical\rquote  projects are more successful in reaching their goals, but this is also due to being more deeply rooted  in cu}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 rent institutions. \par In more detail, Arterton's findings are as follows: \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057  The }{\i\lang2057 private, non-governmental plebiscites}{\lang2057 , aiming to bring \u8222\'e3the people\ldblquote  into politics, are not able to solve \lquote the practical problems of unequal participation and managing political ambition and interest\rquote  (88).\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 The }{\i\lang2057 governmental dialogues}{\lang2057 , aiming to bring the citizens into closer contact with public o}{\lang2057 f}{\lang2057 ficials, are limited in their scope of participation, but this helps against elite manipulation. The participants are able to influence the agenda, because generally it is expected that people are engaged in influencing policy rather than in making decisions. \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 The }{\i\lang2057 governmental plebiscites}{\lang2057 , in which public officials \lquote attempt to learn the will of their co}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 stituents\rquote  (137) although their ultimate authority is maintained, are not very successful with respect to  vo}{\lang2057 t}{\lang2057 er turnout and there remains the question of representation. However, the involvement of public officials by itself appeared to stimulate participation. This obviously affected the legitimacy and credibility of the project and therefore people's belief in the effectiveness of pa}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 ticipation was much stronger.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 The }{\i\lang2057 private, non-governmental dialogues}{\lang2057 , aiming to involve citizens in the public discussion about politics and the community, are the most recent cases of the use of technology (computer confe}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 encing) to improve current democracy. Arterton\rquote s preliminary conclusion from his research in this emerging field is \lquote that computer conferencing will prove to be better suited to communication within established groups than as an instrument through which strangers will engage each other\rquote  (178). He points out that on the one hand only the involvement of public officials would attract people to participate and on the other hand one should be aware that participation in a dialogue takes more time and effort than simply casting a vote.\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par This evaluation shows that some ideas of tele-democracy are more or less related to the i}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 provement of existing representative democratic systems through enhanced citizen particip}{\lang2057 a}{\lang2057 tion, while others somehow imagine  that established democratic processes will be superseded and point towards (more) direct democracy. \par \par Arterton regards tele-democracy projects as most viable if they try to overcome some shortcomings of existing democracy rather than  trying to create a \u8222\'e3new\ldblquote  form. Therefore he explicitly states:\par }\pard\plain \s15\qj\li567\ri1134\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \ldblquote I use teledemocracy to refer, not to politics that would undercut our established representative machi}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 ery, but to the use of communications technology to facilitate the transmission of political information and opinion between citizens and their public leaders.\rdblquote  (14)\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 In contrast to this \lquote conservative\rquote  view of changes in the political system due to technological development, Slaton (1992) imagines a new future of democracy. She presents an idea of tele-democracy suggesting a transition from the (current) \ldblquote expanded representative state\rdblquote  to the \ldblquote representative participatory state\rdblquote , which she calls a \ldblquote quantum step in ideology and theory\rdblquote  (73ff). She uses the term \lquote televote\rquote  which implies the following concept  of tele-democracy:\par }\pard\plain \s15\qj\li567\ri1134\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \ldblquote Like all scientific polling done today, Televote utilizes random sampling based on probability (quantum). Its unique features are: (1) It provides information to respondents - undisputed facts and pro and con arguments (Newtonian); (2) it encourages respondents to discuss the facts, opinions, and issues with others - interaction (quantum); (3) it allows time for deliberation before respondents reply (Newtonian); and (4) it is designed to be used simultaneously with Electronic Town Meetings on the same subject (quantum).\rdblquote  (26)\par \par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 One can see from this definition that \lquote televote\rquote  is in fact a model of democracy which not (only) means direct democracy. It is based on a notion of \lquote true\rquote  representation in the sense of representatives chosen randomly (by lot) from the whole population of citizens. This should give each citizen the same probability to be selected as a representative - independently of gender, age, region, class, race, etc.}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ For a discussion of this kind of direct democracy - which is also called \lquote demarchy\rquote  - in the context of tel}{e}{communications see McLean (1987;158ff).}}}{\lang2057  Apart from the question of representation - or rather sampling (see section 4) - the televote projects are a combination of informing the voters comprehensively and supporting their actual voting. Thus they affect both aspects of tele-democracy: discussion of the topic and voting itself.\par \par The differing perspectives of Slaton and Arterton are also present in their writing about each other. While Arterton discusses the Televote projects of Slaton et al. under  the category of \lquote private, non-governmental plebiscites\rquote , which he considers as not very succes}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 ful in improving citizen participation, Slaton spends a whole chapter of her book on responding to Arterton\rquote s critique of projects under the heading of  \lquote televote\rquote  (Hawaii Televotes), which she consi}{\lang2057 d}{\lang2057 ers to be unjustified in most respects.\par \par These few remarks on tele-democracy projects should indicate that they are concerned with both polling processes, which find out about people's opinions without immediate consequences, and with voting processes, which actually lead to decisions. However, as long as direct democracy appears limited to experimental settings - but also due to its logical bounds (McLean 1989) - efforts to support representatives in learning about the will of the people seem to be more feasible. Opinion polling, which has already significantly changed the workings of established political institutions during  the recent decades}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ see Roszak 1986; 191:\par \uc1\u8222\'e3Over the past decade, as computers have become more proficient and prominent, we have developed a popular culture obsessed with ranking and ratings. Even beauty contests are now elaborately tabulated on esoteric pr}{o}{grams that flash the totals across the television screen from second to second. Sports and athletics have become a morass of instant statistics. The newspapers feature the latest ratings of television shows; movies and records are ranked by their weekly earnings. In the business world, we have day-to-day printouts of corporate perfor}{m}{ance. It was inevitable that this ethos of quantitative competition should finally assimilate politics.\ldblquote }}}{\lang2057 , may become even more important in the age of computer networking (see also section 4).\par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828880}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896599}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011232}3.2. Group Decision-Making{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828880}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896599}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011232}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 If democracy is considered just as a method of taking decisions - \u8222\'e3not a generally benign state of affairs\ldblquote  (McLean 1989; 32) - this connects the questions of largescale and small\_scale decision-making, in other words decision-making in a population or in a group of people. Thinking only of size, one may assume that in decision-making by small groups \lquote direct voting\rquote  proc}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 dures will be  more prominent than representative ones.\par \par }\pard\plain \s15\li567\ri1134\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The support of group decision-making is an important field of application of computer-mediated communication. Speaking of \lquote decision-making\rquote  in the context of CMC, one should  be aware of efforts having quite a long history under the term  \lquote decision support systems\rquote .}{\b\lang2057  Decision Support Systems}{\lang2057  are \lquote interactive computer based systems which help decision makers utilize data and models to solve unstructured problems\rquote  and make decisions. The \lquote system must aid a decision maker in solving unprogrammed, unstructured (or \lquote semistructured\rquote ) problems...the system must possess an interactive query facility, with a query language that ... is ... easy to learn and use. (http://dss.cba.uni.edu/DJP/dssglossary.html)\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 A  recent example of such a decision support system is ZENO (Gordon 1996). ZENO is based on the WWW and uses decision theory and a formal model of argumentation to assist decision makers and concerned parties in public policy-making and with planning procedures. \par \par However, our focus is on decision-making based on knowledge about other people\rquote s prefe}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 ences. This means knowledge about how people rate objects as well as knowledge about how people vote on a subject. Decision support in Web4Groups is envisaged as providing a group of people of any size with a tool to collect, aggregate and analyse the opinions of all members on a particular subject. Therefore we must distinguish between the specific kind of \u8222\'e3decision support\ldblquote  provided by Web4Groups and computer applications known as \lquote decision support systems\rquote . Nevertheless, in some respects, Web4Groups may perform functions similar to  \lquote group decision support systems\rquote .\par }\pard\plain \s15\qj\li567\ri1134\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang1031\cgrid {\b\lang2057 Group Decision Support Systems }{\lang2057 (GDSS) - An interactive, computer-based system that facilitates solution of unstructured problems by a set of decision-makers working together as a group. It aids groups, especially groups of managers, in analysing problem situations and in performing group dec}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 sion making tasks. (http://dss.cba.uni.edu/DJP/dssglossary.html)\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par One may assume that the basic procedure in (small) group decision-making is direct voting. This can take the form of a majority vote, if the vote is on just two options, or a plurality vote, as is usual in cases with more than two options. However, any voting on more than two options can also apply a majority procedure, if voters may rank the options according to their preferences, or if each possible binary combination of options is presented to the voters (more on that below).\par \par The collective outcome of a poll is affected by the \lquote choice system\rquote  used to collect and aggr}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 gate individual preferences (see Urken 1996). A \lquote choice system\rquote  means the way in which an individual may express his or her preference regarding an object, e.g. putting options in some ranking order, scoring options, or simply choosing one option, but the term also means the way how individual prefe}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 ences are aggregated, i.e. determining a \u8222\'e3winner\ldblquote  or computing statistics. Urken (1996) states that \u8222\'e3(i)deally, choice systems should provide decision analysis that guides users in setting up a group decision, in interpreting data as it comes in, and in reaching an inference about the ou}{\lang2057 t}{\lang2057 come.\ldblquote \par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828881}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896600}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011233}3.3. The Mechanics of Voting{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828881}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896600}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011233}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par Thinking about the most democratic way of aggregating individual preferences in an act of collective choice}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ It is important to note that this focus on aggregation implies a notion of democracy which may be contrasted by the model of \lquote deliberative democracy\rquote . London (1995) stresses this point in a comparison of two models of public talk: teledemocracy vs. deliberative democracy.}}}{\lang2057  points to the extensive  literature dealing with \lquote social choice\rquote .\par \par If we want to implement voting mechanisms we must consider a number of factors. There are traditional forms of to arranging a vote which must be considered b}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 cause they are widely used and have the benefit of wide acceptance.\par \par It is, however, undeniable that many of those methods have serious defects. Social choice theory has clarified that for certain situations no rationally defensible unique definition of an aggregation of individual preferences exists which fulfils a number of reasonable condit}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 ons. Those situations were  identified long ago by Charles L. Dodgson, a fellow of Christ Church College in Oxford, who was dissatisfied with the voting procedures in his college. The contr}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 butions of this scholar - who became famous under the pen-name Lewis Carroll  as the author of 'Alice in Wonderland' - were  forgotten for a long time.\par \par In 1951 Kenneth Arrow tried to find a solution to the problem of  fomulating a social welfare fun}{\lang2057 c}{\lang2057 tion, i.e. a procedure aggregating the choices of individuals in an overall evaluation of the pre}{\lang2057 f}{\lang2057 erences of a society or a group of people. He discussed this problem in the context of economic theory: if individuals prefer certain options, what is the best way of representing the preferences of society as a whole? In his book \lquote Social Choice and Individual Values\rquote  (1963) he d}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 scribes his negative findings. He starts with individual orderings of the states of the world by individuals and asks how these orderings should be combined to find a collective social choice. He postulates a number of reasonable conditions for a procedure to achieve such a combination:\par \par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-227\li227\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls18\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls18\adjustright {\lang2057 Collective rationality: assuming that all individuals' orderings are complete (any po}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 sible pair of choices or candidates gets a ranking) and transitive (if a is preferred to b and b is preferred to c, then the individual prefers a to c), then social ordering should have the same properties.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-227\li227\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls18\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls18\adjustright {\lang2057 Universal domain: the social ordering procedure should produce an unambiguous result for every possible configuration of individual preferences.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-227\li227\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls18\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls18\adjustright {\lang2057 Pareto condition: if every individual prefers a to b than the social ordering procedure should rank a over b.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 4.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-227\li227\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls18\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls18\adjustright {\lang2057 Independence condition: the social ranking of any pair of options depends only on the ind}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 vidual rankings of that pair and not on the rankings of either member of the pair against ot}{\lang2057 h}{\lang2057 ers.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f0\lang2057 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-227\li227\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls18\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls18\adjustright {\lang2057 Non-dictatorship: there is no individual such that its preference determines the social prefe}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 ence independently of anybody else's preferences.\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par Arrow then proved that it is logically impossible to satisfy all five conditions simultan}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 ously if more than three people have to decide on more than two options (An example of this situation is shown in the article by Arnold Urken in this volume) . This result shows that there are certain logical limits to democratic procedures. Nevertheless, the situation is not hopeless. In the following we will describe some mechanisms of direct and representative voting. For a fuller picture taking into account arguments from game theory see, for example, Ordeshook (1986).\par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011234}Direct voting{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011234}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 If we have a situation with two options we can take a }{\i\lang2057 simple majority vote}{\lang2057  (or count) to d}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 cide the issue, ignoring abstentions. If there are  more than two options, this cannot be  reduced to a sequence of binary decisions: in general, the result will depend on the order in which the binary decisions are made. \par \par A traditional way out is regularly used in the Scandinavian countries: voting takes place first on the o}{\lang2057 p}{\lang2057 tions with the most far-reaching consequences and then goes on to less demanding sol}{\lang2057 u}{\lang2057 tions. It is clear that this works only in some situations: mainly in those where it remains clear what  the options with the most far-reaching  demands are. Another widely accepted solution is the }{\i\lang2057 pl}{\i\lang2057 u}{\i\lang2057 rality vote}{\lang2057 : voting on all options at once and deciding for the one with the highest count of votes cast. Decisions on candidates are often made by this procedure. This, however, has the drawback that the winning solution may lose against every other option in a pairwise co}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 parison.\par \par Instead one could  try a pairwise comparison of the options: if in each comparison one option gets more votes than all the others the result is clear. The winner of such an exhaustive series of pairwise comparisons is called a }{\i\lang2057 Condorcet}{\lang2057  winner in honour of one of the early theorist of voting pr}{\lang2057 o}{\lang2057 cedures in the modern era. If we have a Condorcet winner this is a fortunate solution. The d}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 bate would be ended. Unfortunately, a Condorcet winner does not necessarily exist. And even worse: if there is none,  a clever choice of majority votes would make any result possible. \par \par More elaborate systems need at least a list of every individual's most preferred options. }{\i\lang2057 Alternative-voting}{\lang2057  proceeds by first counting the first preferences. If one option gets more than half of the votes it has won. Otherwise the option with the smallest number of votes is removed. For those who had preferred this solution their second choice is substituted. This procedure continues until one of the options gets a majority. This method may be used in elections for a single post or in regional elections with a single-member district.\par \par A fundamental problem which leads to the impossibilities of the Arrow theorem is the existence of cycles: a is preferred to b, b to c, c to something else and the last element in the cycle is preferred to a. The most interesting ones are the top cycles: in them, every member of the cycle wins against every option outside the cycle. This allows reducing the number of candidate o}{\lang2057 p}{\lang2057 tions to the members of the top cycle. Social choice theory has shown a number of relationships between the distribution of more than one dimension of preferences and the existence of cycles. But all those results do not yield any  easy recipes for identifying them - or the most preferred options - or to solve further  emerging problems. Cycles tend to point to socially unstable situations. The introduction of further dimensions not previously considered (and changing the importance of dimensions) may sometimes help to resolve cycles over time.Ways out of the Arrow dilemma are ways to break the cycle. Two ways of doing so in a deterministic way and one  statistical solution are described below. Each of them has its drawbacks and may appeal to different constituencies. Ways out of the dilemma, however, have to rely on the cooperative spirit which makes voting as a social mechanism possible in the first place.\par \par One way is the }{\i\lang2057 Borda}{\lang2057  count: This is a ranking order procedure where every voter awards a points to his last choice, a+b to the second last choice, a+2b to the next last choice, and so on. Usually a=1 and b=1. The points awarded by all voters are added up for all options .The winner is the option with the highest sum. This procedure was already described by Nikolaus von Kues (Cusanus) in 1443 (and possibly by Ramon Lull in the 13th cent}{\lang2057 u}{\lang2057 ry) as a method  of electing the Holy Roman E}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 peror) but it received its  name when Jean-Charles de Borda proposed it in 1784 for elections at the Acad\u233\'8emie Royale des Sciences. The problem with this rank procedure is its non-monotonicity: if a voter ranks an option higher, it may happen that the preference for this option decreases. Or, if more persons vote for a winning option it may happen that this option is  not s}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 lected. As monotonicity is implied by the Pareto condition in the Arrow set of conditions, this shows that this requirement is not satisfied by this method. As a consequence voting may in this case be manipulatable. And this is unavoidable. A corollary to the Arrow theorem, the Gibbard-Satterwaith theorem, states that all determinate non-dictatorial social choice procedures are manipulatable. If voters misrepresent their real preferences they may help their first preference or at least prevent their least preferred solution. This possibility for strategic voting was the reason that Borda replied to Condorcet - who directed precisely this critique to him and also to the procedure later named after him - "This procedure is only for honest men".\par \par The other proposal was made  by }{\i\lang2057 Dodgson}{\lang2057 : find the solution breaking the cycle by changing the votes of individuals. The winning option is the one needing the least number of changes of the votes of the individuals to get a cyclefree vote. }{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ cyclicity of individual preferences (instead of transitivity) is not quite enough for a satisfactory result. For an odd number of voters there may still be a 'collegium' which is a group which has to be a part of every winning majority. Members of such a collegium have an increased chance of being decisive. And non collegial choice procedures may still lead to a cyclical social choice. For more on this compare McLean 1989 Appendix; 180)}}}{\lang2057 \par \par At last there is also a non-deterministic solution - or a statistical solution. One may draw an individual - or a group of individuals - and use the resulting preferences to break the cycle. In a way, the individual's or the group's preferences work like a dictatorial solution. But as different individuals may win the lottery at different times, every individual has the same chance to be s}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 lected. He or she also has less incentive to manipulate. Therefore in a certain sense the Arrow cond}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 tions are met.\par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011235}Representative voting{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011235}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Not all preferences are determined directly. The complexity of modern society and of many collective groups, the time constraints of the members of most communities, as well as  political consi}{\lang2057 d}{\lang2057 erations other than the wish for democratically determined decisions make it necessary to install representative forms of democracy. The selection of representatives has a long history and many complex procedures have been devised. From the outset, we may distinguish systems where a number of different districts (or social groups) vote on their representatives by majority or plurality rules, and selection of  a  representative body by a proportional system. Proportional systems make the emergence of political  parties pra}{\lang2057 c}{\lang2057 tically unavoidable. Pure examples of  these two systems  (single seat districts and global propo}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 tional systems) need no further discussion. \par \par The first complication of a vote for a single seat is a double-ballot system: in a first round a plurality vote finds the candidates with the highest support. Only the two candidates with the highest votes enter the final majority vote. Alternative-voting may also be used in this case. In a more complex procedure a Condorcet winner may be sought. If this does not succeed, one of the other methods described above could follow. \par \par For the following  discussion, we will assume that a number of representatives are selected in a 'partial group' of voters and that further seats are distributed by one or more further steps in the procedure. We will speak about the partial group as of voters of a district, knowing that this  need not be a regional division of the electorate. Proportional systems need multi-seat di}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 tricts.\par \par In a }{\i\lang2057 Largest-Remainder}{\lang2057  system, seats using up a predetermined quota of votes in each multi-member district are awarded to parties in proportion to the voters' preferences for a party. The remaining seats are awarded to the parties with the largest remainders of votes (short of the quota). \par \par The limiting case of this is the }{\i\lang2057 Single Transferable Vote}{\lang2057  system. For a district with n seats a quota of V/(n+1) is set where V is the number of votes to be cast. The quota is rounded to the next whole number. Voting is for candidates and those exceeding the quota are elected. The surplus votes are distributed among the other candidates of the same party (by a mechanism to be determined). If nobody reaches the quota this way, the lowest candidate is removed. Further rounds of this fill the available seats.\par \par In a }{\i\lang2057 Highest-Average}{\lang2057  voting system seats in each multi-member district are distributed to pa}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 ties in proportion to the voters' preferences. The remaining seats are awarded by a procedure which successively divides each party's votes by a series of divisors and selects the parties with the highest quotient(s). Thus the party with the highest number of votes wins the first seat. Its number is divided by two and again compared with the votes of the other parties. The highest score wins the next seat. Its party's votes are divided by two, or by three if it was the first winner, and so on. A}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 other way to proceed is to divide the seats of all parties by 2, 3, 4, 5 ... and to look for the n lar}{\lang2057 g}{\lang2057 est quotients (for n seats).\par \par The }{\i\lang2057 Additional Member System}{\lang2057  would distribute two-thirds of the seats by plurality vote in each district, the rest would be used to fill the seats proportionally to the total vote count by the best losers in their respective districts. \par \par Additional rules may exclude parties which do not exceed a given threshold in the overall votes. Only the remaining parties get proportional seats.\par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011236}Some remarks on \lquote collaborative\rquote  rating{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011236}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Since voting was introduced as a special case of rating (see section 1), in turn the findings of s}{\lang2057 o}{\lang2057 cial choice theory on voting may be considered in cases of collaborative rating. In some cases, ratings may be independent of the need for social choice: Most ratings based on measurement are of a different nature. Rating of sports results needs less effort in consensus building. Speeds and heights are not debated socially. But even the points allocated in co}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 petitive games and other forms of competition are loaded with small problems.\par \par Finding  a procedure for \lquote collaborative\rquote  rating of items or persons is equivalent to the d}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 termination of a social welfare function. Thus it is subject to the same impossibility theorems. However, ratings may be performed  by individuals or by small groups of people with similar prefe}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 ence scales. This may help to avoid cycles in most cases (although the faction debates in small groups and societies show that this  is  not always the case). \par \par On the other hand, if ranking is performed  for a large number of items and is directed to a large aud}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 ence with varied interests, only very rough ratings are needed. The audience does not expect a water-tight arguments in all those cases. Thus, a simple count of rankings, or an even more simplified Borda ranking may be sufficient. For some purposes a histogram of the distribution of ratings is sufficient. In other environments, averages or medians (half of the ratings lie below this threshold) are appropriate. Lastly,  the tossing of coins in the case of a tie in some competitions shows that non-deterministic solutions are not alien to this environment.\par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011237}3.4. Conclusions{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011237}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The experience from (large-scale) tele-democracy projects and the need for group-decision support on the one hand, as well as  the findings of social choice theory on the other hand, may be brought together to specify the parameters relevant to  voting procedures.\par \par Procedurally, voting in the narrow sense, i.e. collecting individual votes and aggregating them in a result, is embedded in social contexts of co-operation and competition. Already agenda-setting may occur  in more or less open ways. The "agenda" is the totality of the o}{\lang2057 p}{\lang2057 tions handled and of the issues addressed in an act of voting or in a voting activity. In some environments possible o}{\lang2057 p}{\lang2057 tions may be widely discussed. Public brainstorming would be used in this case to generate a great variety of proposals and the preliminary phase may result in a limited number of pre-selected options. In other settings, a small number of experts, representatives of interest groups, or even randomly chosen juries may prepare a limited set of alternatives. \par \par In both cases the initiation of a vote will put a (possibly temporary) end to discussion. In an open discourse, voting may be used to collect ideas about the general feeling among the group of vo}{\lang2057 t}{\lang2057 ers. In this case a continuous form of voting could be appropriate: it makes the mood of the voting group visible. Already the selection of this group is a design parameter of the vote initiated. In given societal or organisational contexts this parameter can be pre-defined and come up for  revision only occ}{\lang2057 a}{\lang2057 sionally. Where voting drives decisions, the eligibility of individuals to vote may become an important decision in itself.\par \par A number of decisions must be taken and parameters must be fixed in any voting activity. The following list summarises the  essential degrees of freedom embedded in any such activity:\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Voting profile (determined during the  agenda-setting phase or decreed by an initiator)\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Agenda setting and determination of issue(s) to be voted on\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Ordering of alternatives\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Who may initiate a vote?\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Straw vote or decisive vote (definition of the role of voting through discussion or negotiation)\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Definition of the voting group\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Is the delegation of voting rights allowed?\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Definition of confidentiality (open, anonymous, pseudonymous, or choice of the individuals concerned whether  their vote is to  remain secret)\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Determination of security (whether  available and feasible) of\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \tab individual votes\par \tab collective outcome (aggregate result)\par \tab (to protect against falsification, unauthorised access, non-repudiation\par \tab by using time stamps, digital signatures, trusted servers and observers).\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\widctlpar\tx1134{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 May certain rules be changed during ongoing voting? \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\tx709{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl12\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\ilvl12\adjustright {\lang2057 \tab Under what conditions and by which procedures?\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar\tx284\tx851{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Vote-form (specifies one or more questions in a pre-defined or a random order)\par }\pard \qj\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 Answers to questions may fall into several classes:\par -\tab Binary decisions: yes/no decisions or other easily identifiable alternatives \par \tab (increase/decrease, in/out or similar),\par - \tab abstentions are ignored in many cases but may invalidate a vote\par -\tab Multiple choice:\par -\tab select one\par -\tab select approved choices\par -\tab identify unacceptable choices (show stoppers)\par -\tab rank order choices\par -\tab allocate to ordered set of clusters (good, acceptable, indifferent, sufficient, bad)\par }\pard \qj\fi-425\li709\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 -\tab give arithmetic value in an interval (e.g. one to ten); in survey environments: semantic differential\par -\tab allocate a given limited amount of points over the different choices according to preference\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar\tx284\tx851{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Initiation of the vote (technical and social preparation)\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar\tx284\tx851{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Opening of the vote (date/time)\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar\tx284\tx851{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Sending out, distributing and/or publishing voting forms\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar\tx284\tx851{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Casting the vote\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar\tx284\tx851{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Changing the vote (if allowed)\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar\tx284\tx851{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Sending out reminders\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Determination of the periodicity of aggregation\par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \tab once after closing of the vote\par \tab at fixed intervals: every morning, every twenty minutes\par \tab continuously (every time there is a request for an aggregate result)\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls19\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent284\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls19\adjustright {\lang2057 Aggregation algorithm\par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \tab determination of  validity (no duplicate votes, minimum number of voters)\par \tab binary majority\par \tab absolute majority in plurality vote\par \tab plurality (choice with highest number of voters)\par \tab unique majority in pairwise comparison of options (Condorcet solution, needs ran}{\lang2057 k}{\lang2057 ings)\par \tab adding up of rank positions (Borda Count)\par \tab weighted voting (e.g. according to shares in a company, different weights for instit}{\lang2057 u}{\lang2057 tional members in a society)\par \tab ...\par \tab calculation of statistics (percentages, means, moments, median, quartiles, n-tiles) \par \tab plotting of histograms and other graphic representations\par \tab aggregation over all votes or over different classes of voters (multi-step voting)\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls19\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent284\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls19\adjustright {\lang2057 Destruction or preservation of  voting data?\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls19\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent284\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls19\adjustright {\lang2057 Exporting data for further use?\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\tx284\tx851{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 A selected number of combinations of these features may be declared to be the  standard voting profiles. In any of them some parameters (options) will be kept fixed and some options may not be realised at all. Not all options were selected for implementation in the current version of Web4Groups.\par \par \sect }\sectd \linex0\endnhere\sectdefaultcl {\header \pard\plain \s28\qj\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \tqr\tx9072{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\f1\fs20 Surveying in the Social Sciences\tab }{\field{\*\fldinst {\cs38\f1\fs20  PAGE }}{\fldrslt {\cs38\f1\fs20\lang1024 41}}}{\f1\fs20 \par }}\pard\plain \s1\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\lang1031\kerning28\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828883}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896601}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011238}4. A Side Issue: Surveying in the Social Sciences{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828883}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896601}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011238}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par People\rquote s opinions are of common interest to politicians and social scientists. Today the professional polling of people is a major concern not only in marketing but also in politics. In both cases knowledge gained in  the social sciences is applied to obtain valid and reliable i}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 formation on people\rquote s preferences. Statistical representation may even be used directly for p}{\lang2057 o}{\lang2057 litical decision-making (see section 3).\par Computer networks offer new possibilities of conducting surveys. If one wants to use them efficiently their methodological implications must be carefully  examined. The following is a short introduction to some relevant issues.\par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828884}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896602}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011239}4.1. \lquote Electronic\rquote  Questionnaires and Computer-Monitored Data{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828884}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896602}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011239}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Despite the  increasing use of computer networks for  data collection in the social sciences, hardly any relevant literature can be found giving a comprehensive summary of the essential procedures and the problems related to them. The following discussion underlines  the relevance of computer-supported data collection for the established methodological repertoire applied in the social sciences. It starts from the assumption that computers have been used in sociology, psychology, etc. for a long time, but so far mostly for data }{\i\lang2057 processing}{\lang2057  and not for data }{\i\lang2057 collection}{\lang2057 .}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ See the widespread use of computer programs like SPSS (Statistical Package for Social Sciences) supporting quantitative methods but also note a tendency towards computer support in qualitative data analysis.}}}{\lang2057  \par \par Computer supported surveying (i.e. data collection) may be classified  according to }{\i\lang2057 direct}{\lang2057  or }{\i\lang2057 indirect}{\lang2057  acquisition  of data on individuals. In other words, this means a distinction b}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 tween }{\i\lang2057 reactive}{\lang2057  procedures (questionnaire, interview, experiment) and }{\i\lang2057 non-reactive}{\lang2057  procedures (observation). The traditional method of asking people and collecting their answers is a direct way of gaining data, which can be carried out using  computers by simply replacing paper and pencil by a terminal and a screen. In this case we may speak of \lquote electronic questionnaires\rquote . If a computer is used to obtain  data on individuals indirectly by recording certain actions of a (computer) user, this is called \lquote computer-monitored data\rquote . In contrast to electronic questio}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 naires this may happen with or without the consent of the persons observed. Such individuals are passive with respect to the data collection - they do not give answers -, but they are active with respect to a certain activity that is investigated. \par \par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 1. In the relevant literature the variety of electronic questionnaires is often discussed under the heading  \lquote computer-assisted interviewing\rquote  (CAI). De Leeuw et al. (1995) define CAI as follows:\par }\pard\plain \s15\qj\li567\ri1134\sb120\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Characteristic for all forms of computer-assisted interviewing is that questions are read from the co}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 puter screen, and the responses are entered directly in the computer, either by an interviewer or by a r}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 spondent. (326)\par }\pard\plain \qj\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The most common forms of CAI are: computer-assisted telephone interviewing (CATI), co}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 puter-assisted personal interviewing (CAPI) and computer-assisted self-interviewing (CASI). While CATI and CAPI still require a person asking the questions and collecting the answers, the respondents of CASI are using the computer themselves. The CASI method is employed in most of the surveys carried out in computer networks.\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 A further distinction regarding electronic questionnaires is that between }{\i\lang2057 stand alone}{\lang2057  and }{\i\lang2057 online}{\lang2057  applic}{\lang2057 a}{\lang2057 tions. Stand alone means that the computer is only used by the respondent for answering the questions. This method is already widely used in empirical research and it is well documented  in the literature (e.g. de Leeuw et al. 1995). In online questionnaires, however, the computer is also used to distribute the questionnaire to the respondents and to collect their replies, provided that both the computers of the researcher and the respondents are connected to a network (e.g. Internet).}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ \lquote Online\rquote  in this context only means that the respondents receive the questionnaire through the connection of their computers to a network. The answering itself, however, can be done without being connected, i.e. offline.}}}{\lang2057  Those questionnaires are  becoming increasingly  widespread, since the number of Internet users has recently increased significantly. Therefore, there has not yet been much methodological discussion, but some practical tips on how to conduct online surveys are already available (e.g. Batinic 1996).\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 2. Computer-monitored data (CMD) are obtained  by  - in most cases - unobtrusive observation of individuals. This method is unobtrusive since  data collection is performed aut}{\lang2057 o}{\lang2057 matically by the system being used. Williams et al (1988) define CMD as \u8222\'e3information collected automatically by a computer about the flow, type, content, or time of interaction by a person using the system\ldblquote  (91). \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 As in the case of  electronic questionnaires, the applicability of this method depends on access to co}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 puters or even computer networks; both of which are  currently distributed unevenly in the populations of a city, a country, or larger entities. Moreover, it is only possible to observe certain activities of computer use. \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 The analysis of so-called \lquote logfiles\rquote  has become the most common application of CMD. Those are files where  the computer automatically stores information on some pre-set variables (e.g. date, time, user name, etc.) every time it is used . On the one hand, there are logfiles stored as a ma}{\lang2057 t}{\lang2057 ter of routine - e.g. on each Internet server -, but on the other hand logfiles may be specified for a survey. Routine (standard) logfiles are easily available, but in this case the specification of data is given. Nevertheless, social science investigations mostly rely on this form of CMD.}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ There can be imagined many uses of CMD in different contexts, e.g. \uc1\u8222\'e3surveillance\ldblquote  of employees in corporate networks.}}}{\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 The great advantage of CMD lies  in the high reliability of such data. Williams et al. (1988; 92ff) note that due to the unobtrusiveness of the instrument the data are more reliable than with self-reports by investigated persons. However the validity of CMD must  be scrutinised car}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 fully since, for example, the logon time of a user may be different from his or her active time of use. \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 It is important to note that electronic questionnaires and computer-monitored data are not only different methods of computer-supported data collection, but that they can be combined successfully. More than the use of just one method, it is their combination which allows interesting research designs (see Abrams & Williams 1996).\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828885}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896603}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011240}4.2. Problems of Sampling{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828885}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896603}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011240}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Electronic questionnaires distributed in computer networks are a highly efficient means for investigating certain populations. But like in traditional empirical research the problem how to draw a sample remains. The basic question is: does the researcher know what the population}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ Here we speak only of \lquote population\rquote  and do not distinguish between \lquote general\rquote  and \lquote working\rquote  population.\par \uc1\u8222\'e3The general population is the total population to which the researchers sees his results as relevant.\ldblquote  (McC.Miller & Wilson 1983; 54)\par \u8222\'e3The working population is the population from which the researcher actually draws his sample.\ldblquote  (ibidem; 121)}}}{\lang2057  of a survey is? If this cannot be determined exactly, the self-selection of respondents must be considered as a harmful  influence in drawing  a representative sample. There are several possible ways of  drawing a sample. The problems caused by  self-selection vary  depending on the scope and the method of the  survey in question. \par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011241}How to{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011241}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 First of all one must  decide if the population can be determined or not. In a na}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 row statistical sense (see Friedrichs 1980; 128ff) a sample is only possible if the population is defined. But in the following discussion we may also speak of a \lquote sample\rquote  if a certain number of persons  is drawn from an unknown population. Therefore, more generally, every group of people analysed by researchers may be regarded as a sample if the findings refer to more persons than the ones i}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 vestigated.\par Every sample is subject to a certain selection procedure. Those procedures differ according to the probability of each member of a population to become part of the sample. Either each individual is  selected at random, or some individuals are privileged, because they are selected intentio}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 ally - due to the will of the researcher or the individual itself. Any random sample}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ This is \uc1\u8222\'e3(a) sample in which all members of the population have a known chance of selection, this chance, for any population member, being greater than zero and less than one (i.e. certainty).\ldblquote  (McC.Miller & Wilson 1983; 91)}}}{\lang2057  requires a definition of the population. \par \par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 1. Surveys with an }{\i\lang2057 undefined}{\lang2057  population are quite common on the Internet (e.g. user su}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 veys}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ E.g. GVU\rquote s user surveys at http://www.cc.gatech.edu/gvu/user_surveys/User_Survey_Home.html}}}{\lang2057 ). In those surveys sampling depends on the effort of distributing the questionnaire as widely as possible. Therefore the size of the sample depends on the period of data collection and on  knowledge about  the questionnaire among Internet users. \par }\pard \qj\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par There are several ways of distributing a questionnaire on the Internet: sending it directly to ind}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 viduals via e-mail, posting it in newsgroups or mailing lists, or promoting a webpage containing a questionnaire (e.g. announcements via e-mail, links from other web pages, registration by search engines). \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 The strategy of large distribution is based on the assumption that with an undefined population one may only obtain relevant findings if one has  a large sample. Such surveys usually investigate  typical characteristics of Internet users (e.g. demographics, usage, dispositions). Accor}{\lang2057 d}{\lang2057 ingly, interpretation of the results aims to present details on the members of the \lquote Internet community\rquote . However, in most cases, there seems to be some awareness that systematic di}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 tortions in representing the population cannot be avoided. \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 2. Surveys with a }{\i\lang2057 defined}{\lang2057  population are obviously carried out less often, but they are usually accompanied by a methodological discussion (e.g. Kiesler & Sproull 1986; Walsh et al 1992; Anderson & Gansneder 1995). \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 If the population is defined, a random sample may be drawn, which allows one to make repr}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 sentative statements about a population by statistical inference. It is possible to determine the population in computer network surveys, if the users of a local network (e.g. a campus network) or the subscribers of a mailing list are investigated. In both cases, all members can  somehow be identified.}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ Normally at least the administrator of a local network or a mailing list has access to a list of users or su}{b}{scribers.}}}{\lang2057  \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\li284\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 However, it often happens that the population of a survey is determined by using  CMD (computer-monitored data). This method takes specific information of the logfiles to select certain persons forming the population of a survey. A common way is to check the fr}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 quency of using the system in the logfiles and thus generating a population of frequent users. In the course of a more sophisticated recording of user-specific information, some other selections can  be imagined.}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ This leads to  some unsolved questions of the protection of privacy. Currently many related aspects are discussed under the term  \lquote cookies\rquote .}}}{\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par The following are three examples of using  CMD to determine the population of a survey:\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Kiesler & Sproull (1986) filtered out all people who had frequently used the system during the previous three days from the logfiles of the Carnegie Mellon University co}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 puter system. They drew a random sample from this population for answering an electronic questionnaire.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Walsh et al. (1992) conducted stratified sampling from the subscribers of an oceanogr}{\lang2057 a}{\lang2057 pher\rquote s mailing list. A divided random sample was drawn from the sub-populations of \lquote above average\rquote  and \lquote below average\rquote  users, which were determined from the frequency of use in the logfiles.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\lang2057 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-283\li283\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls10\adjustright {\lang2057 Anderson & Gansneder (1995) selected from the users of the Cleveland Free-Net \lquote active\rquote  persons  who had logged into the system during  the previous two weeks. From this pop}{\lang2057 u}{\lang2057 lation a random sample was drawn for a survey in an electronic questionnaire via e-mail.\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011242}Self-selection{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011242}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 In social science surveys self-selection may occur in two ways: either from a sample  or from a population (defined or not). Every sampling procedure is concerned with the first kind of self-selection, because some persons selected to form a sample may not be willing to participate in the  survey.}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ Although people\rquote s self selection from a sample is usually discussed with respect to questionnaires, a refusal to participate in an investigation may also occur in interviews or in experiments. Empirical research in the social sciences always depends on the willingness of the people under scrutiny.}}}{\lang2057  Only a few  sampling procedures involve the second kind of self-selection, namely those where }{\i\lang2057 each}{\lang2057  member of a population is allowed - or rather encouraged - to choose to participate in a survey. \par \par The problem with self-selection is that due to a certain sampling procedure some part of a population (defined or not) may be systemat}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 cally misrepresented. While the reason for this clearly is people's varying willingness to participate in a survey, one may only speculate about the individual motivations which can hardly be determined. \par \par The influence of self-selection on the results of a survey is well documented in the literature on empirical methodology discussing the characteristics of investigations as well as of people participating voluntarily (e.g. Bortz/D\u246\'9aring 1995; 71ff). However it is not clear to what extent those findings may be applied to surveys using computer networks.\par In principle, one can say that the problem of self-selection is bigger if the population of a su}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 vey is undefined. A defined population may be described to a certain extent, which allows one to make assumptions about self-selection due to certain characteristics of the persons involved (e.g. level of education). Most often self-selection is discussed as a problem in surveys aiming to distri}{\lang2057 b}{\lang2057 ute a questionnaire as widely as possible to an undefined population.\par \par Walsh et al. (1992) tried to compare a random sample with a sample resulting from people's self-selection . In a computer network survey a questionnaire was distributed via e-mail directly to a random sample on the one hand, and on the other hand it was made available on a bulletin board.}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ Messages posted on a bulletin board may be read by every  user of a network.}}}{\lang2057  Walsh et al. found that self-selected respondents are willing to spend more time and money on answering the questionnaire. They also have a more positive attitude towards using of computer networks, and they see the advantages rather than the disadvantages of ele}{\lang2057 c}{\lang2057 tronic communication. Although those are not the results of a study dedicated to the problem of self-selection, they emphasise some assumptions about the characteristics of response in computer networks.\par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011243}Response{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011243}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 As shown above, the problem of response is caused by people\rquote s self-selection from a sample. Not all members of a population being approached to participate in a survey are willing to do so. That means, the number of completed questionnaires is smaller than the number of questionnaires distributed. Therefore a so-called \lquote response rate\rquote  is usually calculated to express the pe}{\lang2057 r}{\lang2057 centage of the sample chosen that  actually participated. \par \par The calculation of a response rate is easy if the size of the sample, i.e. the number of questionnaires distri}{\lang2057 b}{\lang2057 uted, is known. This is the case in computer network surveys where the que}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 tionnaires are distributed to individual persons via e-mail, e.g. using a mailing list. But if the que}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 tionnaire is stored at one place and may be accessed by many people (e.g. in the Usenet or the World Wide Web), then it is not easy to determine the actual number of \u8222\'e3distributed\ldblquote  questionnaires. Currently it is hardly possible in the Usenet to find out how many people r}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 trieved a certain message. In the World Wide Web, however, there are ways of at least getting a rough idea, namely by analysing the logfiles.}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ Uncertainties are caused by users accessing a page which is not counted in the original logfile, for example due to the use of a \lquote proxy\rquote .}}}{\lang2057  A response may be determined by calculating the rate of the number of submitted forms in the number of retrieved pages, both of which may be derived from the logfiles.\par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828886}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896604}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011244}4.3. Conclusion{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828886}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896604}s{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011244}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The nature of the response in a survey is an important issue in  interpreting the results. \par As regards voting and rating activities in Web4Groups, it is necessary to consider two types of surveys which may be performed:\par }\pard \qj\sb120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 1. surveys which collect people\rquote s opinions on a specific topic at a specific time (voting)\par }\pard \fi-284\li284\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 2. \lquote surveys\rquote  which collect people\rquote s opinions on every topic they choose at any time they choose (rating).\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\lang2057 In the first case, it is most important to analyse the response, because this informs us about particip}{\lang2057 a}{\lang2057 tion in a vote, but also enables the elimination of  irregular responses (e.g. multiple voting). In the second case, it could be particularly interesting to keep records of the individual responses in order to develop a profile of long-run preferences of Web4Groups users (see collab}{\lang2057 o}{\lang2057 rative rating).\par \sect }\sectd \pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\margtsxn1440\linex0\endnhere\sectdefaultcl {\header \pard\plain \s28\qj\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \tqr\tx9072\tqr\tx14034{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\f1\fs20 Voting and Rating Agents\tab }{\field{\*\fldinst {\cs38\f1\fs20  PAGE }}{\fldrslt {\cs38\f1\fs20\lang1024 48}}}{\f1\fs20 \par }}\pard\plain \s1\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\lang1031\kerning28\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828887}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896605}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011245}5. Voting and Rating Agents for Web4Groups{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828887}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896605}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011245}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par This section describes  design guidelines for integrating a voting and a rating functionality in the Web4Groups service portfolio. It starts with a description of general design principles in Web4Groups and attempts to show how voting and rating agents will communicate with the distributed Web4Groups messaging database, making only a few changes to it. This section is not a full specification document, but tries to touch upon all major design questions. The design guidelines are based on theoretical investigations and an analysis of scenarios worked out by archetypal user groups.\par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828888}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896606}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011246}5.1. The Web4Groups Service{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828888}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896606}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011246}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Web4Groups allows any Internet user to join or start a public forum or a closed workspace in order to exchange messages or multimedia documents (such as word processing files or calcul}{\lang2057 a}{\lang2057 tion tables). All objects, which may be either activities or messages, are called "boards". Boards are made accessible not only via the WorldWideWeb, but also via e\_mail (where they could replace list servers for mailing lists) as well as via fax or telephone. \par \par }\pard\plain \s18\sb120\sa120\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Ref382644646}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382800927}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011348}Figure }{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\lang2057  SEQ Figure \\* ARABIC }}{\fldrslt {\lang1024 1}}}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Ref382644646}: Web4Groups service architecture{\*\bkmkend _Toc382800927}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011348}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvl