tions in accordance with the needs of the group. As the field of application of Web4Groups ranges from open discussions, market situations, project archives, educational support to guest books, customisable role assignment could help to better serve such a variety of applications. \par \par Possible ways of handling roles were investigated, discussed and described in the paper 'Social Functions in Virtual Communities' (Alton-Scheidl, Diallo, Tscherteu 1996). The paper pr}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 sented a definition of a set of principal role elements, i.e. the right to read, create or write in a specific board. A set of role elements may define both the behaviour of a board for a visitor and for all possible roles. Thus, the role concept makes Web4Groups open for further application settings. \par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011247}Technical architecture{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011247}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Web4Groups builds on a client-server architecture. While the server side of the system was clear from the earliest phase of the project, the consortium had to consider which clients should be used. Eventually it opted for using a web browser supporting HTML 2 as the pr}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 mary client, and to have additional e-mail, telnet and fax client gat}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 ways. \par \par The server is the place where the data on all objects involved in the system are stored. These objects may be boards (forums, workspaces, messages, etc.) or users. The server consists of a database containing the objects of the system and a server process which receives requests and performs the corresponding operation. The database is associated with a database demon which monitors and reorganises the database according to given criteria. \par \par The conferencing system distributed uses a communication protocol between each server, i}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 ternally known as the KOM protocol. This protocol builds on the TCP/IP protocol. The server also provides gateways to SMTP and NNTP so that users may directly access E-mail and Usenet news servers from within the Web4Groups co}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 ferencing system.\par One of the major characteristics of the Web4Groups conferencing server is its 'distributedness', which means that many servers may co-operate to provide a coherent Web4Groups service. Every user has a dedicated/?/, associated server. But a user can access any object in the system, no matter on which server this object is based. Every object in the database has one master server, and if a particular object is not available in the local server, it is fetched from its master server. Information is replicated as needed between servers. For example, a forum is replicated aut}{\lang2057 o}{\lang2057 matically to all servers where that forum has at least one member. The forum description is additionally replicated to all servers where there is an announc}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 ment of this forum. The architecture of Web4Groups is shown in Figure 5. The arrows indicate the main directions of information tran}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 fer. \par \par }\pard\plain \s18\sb120\sa120\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382800928}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011349}Figure }{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\lang2057  SEQ Figure \\* ARABIC }}{\fldrslt {\lang1024 2}}}{\lang2057 : Flow of information between Web4Groups servers and clients{\*\bkmkend _Toc382800928}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011349}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\f36\fs20\lang2057 {\pict{\*\picprop\shplid1026{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}