. \par \par With the suggested way of implementation, the Web4Groups service structure distributed can be used for the following: voters can be registered on different servers and they can even participate using the e-mail gateway. What will be added to Web4Groups is a new type of activity: \lquote voting\rquote  with corr}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 sponding access rights and parameters. The voting agent who will start, announce or aggregate a voting activity, will communicate with the Web4Groups system via IP using its API library; so there will be no big changes to the database.\par \par Any user allowed to start an activity may start a voting activity. By default, all me}{\lang2057 m}{\lang2057 bers of the parent activity will be inherited. The creator of the activity is its organiser and he or she may use the interfaces already available in Web4Groups to add or remove members from this activity. The voting agent need not worry  much about voters as this aspect is dealt with by  the Web4Groups' membership administration.\par \par Voting procedure is described in a voting script. This is a text file, which is stored as a special MIME part as part of a voting activity. Each vote is a message sent to the voting activity. The result of the voting procedure is stored as a special MIME type in a new message sent to the voting activity; all voting inputs may optionally be stored in a separate  file for further calc}{\lang2057 u}{\lang2057 lation with separate tools. A message containing a vote may be replaced by the voter with the Web4Groups supersede mechanism, if this option is set.\par \par }\pard\plain \s18\sb120\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382800930}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011351}Figure }{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\lang2057  SEQ Figure \\* ARABIC }}{\fldrslt {\lang1024 4}}}{\lang2057 : MIME types holding voting information{\*\bkmkend _Toc382800930}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011351}\par }\pard\plain \qj\ri4394\widctlpar\box\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Content-Type: text/Web4Groups-VoteScript\par Content-Type: text/Web4Groups-VoteResult\par Content-Type: text/Web4Groups-Vote\par Content-Type: text/Web4Groups-VoteInputs\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par Depending on the voting procedure selected and the parameters chosen, the possible answers are set and users are notified before the closing of a vote, or other users may see one's voting or not. The following is  an example of a voting activity and its messages.\par \par }\pard\plain \s18\sb120\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382800931}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011352}Figure }{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\lang2057  SEQ Figure \\* ARABIC }}{\fldrslt {\lang1024 5}}}{\lang2057 : Example of a voting activity and its messages{\*\bkmkend _Toc382800931}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011352}\par }\pard\plain \qj\ri3118\widctlpar\box\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\fs18\lang2057 1. Parts of an activity of type "Voting":\par \par ---Subject---\par "Vote on the moon's physics" \par ---Text---\tab \par "Today it is full moon and we would like to hear your opinion!" \par ---Web4Groups-VoteScript---\par StartQuestion\par Is the moon made of blue cheese?\par EndQuestion\par VotingModel=BinaryChoice\par Options="yes":"no"\par AnonymousVote=no\par VoteOpens=now\par VoteCloses=1997:05:01\par Invite=yes\par Remind=no\par \par 2. Parts of a message containing a vote:\par \par ---Subject---\par "My spectacular vote" \par ---Text---\par "I rather believe it is made of Emmental cheese." \par ---Web4Groups-Vote---\par "Vote=yes"\par \par 3. Parts of the message containing the result:\par \par ---Subject---\par "Voting Results on: vote on the moon's physics" \par ---Text---\par "Comment by XYZ: I rather believe it is made of Emmental cheese." \par ---Web4Groups-VoteResult---\par "yes=34\par no=897\par abstain=4"\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par A voting procedure must be able to handle several questions, so that votes can be concatenated to an inquiry with several parts. This can be implemented in two ways: either by starting a new voting activity for each question and performing the concatenation on the user interface side or by packing all questions into one voting activity. The latter makes the voting definition la}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 guage more complex, but makes it easier to set up inquiries with several questions. The a}{\lang2057 d}{\lang2057 vanced Web4Groups service will preferably support multiple questions in one voting proc}{\lang2057 e}{\lang2057 dure.\par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011253}Voting phases{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011253}\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Start-up \par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 In the start-up phase, the voting procedure is defined by starting a new voting activity. Que}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 tions are set, voting models are assigned, parameters are set. The creator of the activity will be its organiser and may change parameters or questions. In this phase, users may create simple messages and replies in the voting activity to discuss the agenda or the voting model applied. However, the organiser is responsible for generating a script defining the voting procedure. The script is stored as a MIME part of the voting activity. In a discussion, only the organiser will be able to revise a voting description by using the supersede/replace mechanism. However, the organiser may nominate others to be  organisers. It will be possible to use the web and the e-mail interface for defining the voting script. \par \par Members are defined like in any other Web4Groups activity. By default, all members of the activity from where the voting activity originated will be inherited . The same access controls will be applied as in the parent activity (open / closed) and may be changed by its organiser using the "board features" dialogue. Web4Groups stores rights as so-called role-elements. Thus, it is necessary to introduce three new role elements: (1) the right to vote, (2) the right to read votes and (3) the right to start and configure a voting procedure. In the simpl}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 fied role model we are currently using in Web4Groups, (1) coincides with the right to write a message or create a board, (2) with the right to read a message and (3) with the orga}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 iser rights.\par \par As soon as the voting activity is created, the voting agent is notified and waits for the starting date of the vote. \par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Announcement\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 On the starting date of the vote, the voting agent will send voting forms to external e-mail users. From that moment, the web UI gateway will show voting forms and apply the appropriate access rules, if someone clicks the voting activity. Members of the voting activity are notified automatically anyway, as they receive the voting activity in their subscribed activities board, where they can easily take note of any new board.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Pending \par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 A user who accesses the voting activity via the web UI gateway, will see a form with questions and possibilities to answer. A user who receives a voting form by e-mail may reply to the Web4Groups mail server and fill in the answers using the e-mail client. In this phase, the vo}{\lang2057 t}{\lang2057 ing procedure cannot be changed by the organiser; and s/he will only be able to submit the vote. Depending on a certain parameter, a voter may change the vote, which is handled internally in the Web4Groups database as the  replacement of a message. If the vote is open and conti}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 uous, a result is calculated every time a new vote arrives. The agent creates a new message in the voting activity that holds the voting result. The result may be replaced by updated voting calculations. Whether other voters are allowed to see the votes also depends on a given p}{\lang2057 a}{\lang2057 rameter.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Reminder\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 If such a parameter is set, the voting agent will remind members once or repeatedly to cast their vote. In a continuos vote, all members are reminded, otherwise only those who have not yet submitted their vote.\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\i\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Closure of Voting  \par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The agent checks the closing date and then calculates the final voting result according to the given voting model. Depending on the set parameters, the result may include information on who cast what kind of  vote. Comments by voters are also listed in the voting result message.\par \par The figure below presents  an example of a possible user interface showing the phases of a voting procedure from the organiser's viewpoint.\par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011254}Voting models{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011254}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 A voting model is the combination of a certain choice model with a corresponding procedure for aggregating individual preferences to determine a collective \u8222\'e3winner\ldblquote . Taking into account the insights gained from  theories of voting and rating (see chapter 3) as well as our knowledge of  the practices of everyday life (see chapter 2) a list of choice models can be developed. Each choice model embodies a specific way how individuals express their preferences on a subject.\par \par Choice models:\par \par A }{\i\lang2057 binary choice}{\lang2057  allows one to answer a question by simply choosing between two alternatives. In most cases those alternatives will be yes or no.\par \par Example: Should the broken light bulb be changed?\par }\pard \qj\li1418\widctlpar\adjustright {\b\lang2057 Yes}{\lang2057 \par No\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par A }{\i\lang2057 single selection choice}{\lang2057  allows one to answer a question by choosing one of several options. Those options will usually present different alternatives.\par \par Example: How should the broken light bulb be changed?\par }\pard \qj\li993\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 You may select (only) one option.\par }\pard \qj\li1418\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 Use a screwdriver\par Ask a committee\par }{\b\lang2057 Call an ambulance}{\lang2057 \par Hire a consultant\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par A }{\i\lang2057 multiple selection choice}{\lang2057  allows one to answer a question by choosing one or more of se}{\lang2057 v}{\lang2057 eral options.\par \par Example: How should the broken light bulb be changed?\par }\pard \qj\li993\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 You may select all options which apply in your opinion.\par }\pard \qj\li1418\widctlpar\adjustright {\b\lang2057 Use a screwdriver\par }{\lang2057 Ask a committee\par }{\b\lang2057 Call an ambulance}{\lang2057 \par Hire a consultant\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par A }{\i\lang2057 ranking choice}{\lang2057  allows one to answer a question by putting several options in a ranking order. \par The full preference schedule of an individual is shown. \par \par Example: How should the broken light bulb be changed?\par }\pard \qj\li993\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 You may put all options in a ranking order.\par }\pard \qj\li1418\widctlpar\adjustright {\b\lang2057 2}{\lang2057  Use a screwdriver\par }{\b\lang2057 4}{\lang2057  Ask a committee\par }{\b\lang2057 1}{\lang2057  Call an ambulance\par }{\b\lang2057 3}{\lang2057  Hire a consultant\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par An }{\i\lang2057 evaluation choice}{\lang2057  is essentially a number of single selection choices on each option. This means that each option may be assigned }{\fs22\lang2057 (only) }{\lang2057 one of several categories. In most cases those categories will express a degree of approval or relevance.\par \par Example: How should the broken light bulb be changed?\par }\pard \qj\li993\sa240\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 You may select a degree of approval for each option.\par }\trowd \trgaph70\trleft284\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw15 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw30 \cltxlrtb \cellx2268\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cltxlrtb \cellx3828\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cltxlrtb \cellx4962\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cltxlrtb \cellx6237\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cltxlrtb \cellx7513\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw30 \cltxlrtb \cellx9214\pard \qj\widctlpar\intbl\adjustright {\lang2057 Use a screwdriver\cell }\pard \q