par\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ http://obelix.soe.oeaw.ac.at/w4g/user/b-4.html, http://www.Web4Groups.at/w4g/user/stat5.html}}}{\lang2057  Users want to have  the possibility to use ratings for filtering information. Ratings may be given by selected users only (e.g. peer reviewers) or by all users. \par \par Within the scope of this project a simplified rating service is specified to support users in finding useful information within high-volume discussion forums. For small groups using their own wor}{\lang2057 k}{\lang2057 spaces, the Web4Groups Voting service will be the preferred tool in decision-making. Ho}{\lang2057 w}{\lang2057 ever, the rating facility will be available in any Web4Groups activity. The specification is open for further rating applications like collaborative filtering}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ Collaborative rating is a way of comparing users' tastes/attitudes and then make suggestions to users with similar tastes/attitudes.}}}{\lang2057 .\par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011258}Rating dimensions{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011258}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 What kinds of categories or dimensions must be considered (quality, violence, decency, comprehensibility, usefulness for group A, B, C, etc.)? Which labels have to be considered? Must the labels be put in some order? Is there a numeric scale (e.g. a standard range from 1 to 100)?\par \par The current project focuses on one dimension: users are to rate "quality" with a range from 0 (poor) to 5 (superb). The suggested icons for representing quality are stars (1-5) and a trash container (0). The ratings are simply added up; the number of stars will relate to the means of ratings.\par \par However, the internal data structure will be able to handle any dimension or labels. The PICS documentation and implementation guidelines}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ http://www.w3.org/pub/WWW/PICS/\par Rating labels are specified at http://www.w3.org/pub/WWW/PICS/labels.html}}}{\lang2057  contain  the label list syntax definition below.\par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011259}Who may rate?{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011259}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 It is  supposed that in many cases the rating will not be established by a single person but by a larger group or by organisations. It is assumed that each of those persons will make a "rating contribution" which will be merged into an "aggregate rating" (the result of the rating).\par \par Web4Groups registers  rights as so-called role-elements. three new role elements must be introduced: (1) the right to give ratings, (2) the right to read ratings and (3) the right to configure ratings. In the simplified role model currently used in Web4Groups (1) coincides with the right to write a message or create a board, (2) with the right to read a me}{\lang2057 s}{\lang2057 sage and (3) with the organiser rights. Anonymous users will not be able to rate, as there is no user object which could hold the rating link to a rated board. Weights are special user attri}{\lang2057 b}{\lang2057 utes expressing the amount of expertise or authority of the participant in the rating process; but the consideration of weights lies outside the scope of the current project.\par \par A minimum number of rating visitors will often be postulated. Also a certain fraction (quota or absolute number) of all those eligible for the rating process could be necessary before a rating is announced. This will be configurable in the "board features dialogue".\par \par Many existing rating systems allow making a new rating at every visit. Others allow only one co}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 tribution by every member of the eligible group. Web4Groups can identify participants by their e-mail address, or by their Web4Group name and can restrict rating contributions on that basis. In Web4Groups, a user may rate the same object more than once, but then the older rating will be replaced.\par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011260}Incentives{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011260}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 When designing a rating facility in which every user is allowed to give ratings, one must  think of giving  users incentives to encourage participation. One possibility would be  that a user receiving  many points would somehow benefit by being given  more rights within the Web4Groups service. A complex game in which roles are assigned to rating budgets is described  in Appendix 1. Ho}{\lang2057 w}{\lang2057 ever, for the current scope of the project simpler rating procedures are applied. Still, the problem of incentives remains. Therefore it is  suggested to simply calculate each user's score for ratings earned and find a way for publicising  this score (e.g. when showing members of an activity). The possibility of  becoming or electing a "VIP" with many good ratings may be a good  incentive for many users to use the rating facility. A service provider might give a prize to  the person with the highest score (free Internet access for a month, etc.). Aggregating scores is very easy to implement and could help making the rating service a "common good".\par  \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011261}Distributing ratings using PICS}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ http://www.w3.org/pub/WWW/PICS/}}}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Toc384011261}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 PICS is an instrument exclusively for  transmitting results of ratings. It clearly addresses the need to distinguish many different rating services (communities, groups, companies, individuals) and different ways of representing the results.\par \par Three different forms of transmissions are defined:\par 1. Ratings included in the META tag of an HTML header\par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 2. Via http from a label bureau using an HTTP query (with documents a PICS enabled server is needed), or from a separate bureau\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 3. In RFC-822 headers along with the document \par \par The new MIME content type "application/pics-label:<labellist>" will be used to store rating results in Web4Groups objects.\par \par Each path may be used to transmit the "content labels"\par 1. An URL naming the rating service that produced the label\par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 2. A set of PICS-defined attribute-value pairs describing information about the rating (the date ra}{\lang2057 t}{\lang2057 ing was assigned)\par 3. A set of rating-system-defined attribute-value pairs which actually rate the item along var}{\lang2057 i}{\lang2057 ous dimensions (categories).\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par Together they form the PICS label distribution. Ratings are provided by a PICS "rating ser}{\lang2057 v}{\lang2057 ice" described by an application/pics service document type.\par \par Normally the different services may use differing "rating systems": they use different dime}{\lang2057 n}{\lang2057 sions, different scales of label values on these dimensions and different criteria in assigning the values. In general, a PICS label also points to icons to be used to depict the essence of the label. Thus it is very well suited to describe the result of any (most?) rating process(es) in Web4Groups.\par }{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\lang2057  REF _Ref382644646 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt {\lang2057 Figure 1111}}}{\lang2057  shows the syntax definition for <labellist> in modified BNF to hold rating results for objects:\par \par Web4Groups will offer the functionality of a rating service bureau for its own pages. \par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011262}How to show and store rating results{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011262}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Quality ratings are to  help a user to determine whether it is worth reading a message or not }{\i\lang2057 b}{\i\lang2057 e}{\i\lang2057 fore}{\lang2057  selecting it for reading. Therefore, the quality rating information will  be visible in the outline view, and it will  certainly be possible to  see them in the board view again, when reading the full message text. To make the quality rating visible in the outline view, the rating information of a board must be stored in the database in such a way that it can be accessed quickly when showing only its subject. Therefore, the quality rating information may be stored as a separate integer field as part of the board, in addition to storing it as a separate  MIME part of the message. However, the <labellist> MIME part may also hold rating dimensions other than quality. A decision on how to optimise access to quality rating is up to the implementers; using a separate integer field is only a suggestion.\par }{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\lang2057  REF _Ref382644785 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt {\lang2057 Figure 10101010}}}{\lang2057  shows the mock-up of a sample  Web4Groups page containing ratings of the activity itself and its messages.\par }\pard\plain \s18\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \page {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382800935}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011356}Figure }{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\lang2057  SEQ Figure \\* ARABIC }}{\fldrslt {\lang1024 9}}}{\lang2057 : Syntax for <labellist> to represent PICS compliant rating results{\*\bkmkend _Toc382800935}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011356}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\box\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \pvpara\posy6\absw7615\dxfrtext142\dfrmtxtx142\dfrmtxty0\nowrap\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\fs16\lang2057 labellist :: '(' version service-info+ ')'\par version :: 'PICS-1.1'\par service-info :: 'error' '(no-ratings' explanation* ')'\par               | serviceID service-error | serviceID option* labelword label*\par serviceID :: quotedURL\par labelword :: 'labels' | 'l'\par label :: label-error | single-label | '(' single-label* ')'\par single-label :: option* ratingword '(' rating+  ')'\par ratingword :: 'ratings' | 'r'\par quotedURL :: '"' URL '"' as described and extended in Rating Services and Rating Systems.\par option :: labeloption | documentoption | otheroption\par labeloption ::\par           'by' quotedname\par         | 'generic' boolean          | 'gen' boolean \par         | 'for' quotedURL\par         | 'on' quoted-ISO-date        \par         | 'signature-RSA-MD5' "base64-string"\par         | 'until' quoted-ISO-date    | 'exp' quoted-ISO-date\par documentoption ::\par           'at' quoted-ISO-date        \par         | 'MIC-md5' "base64-string"  | 'md5' "base64-string"\par otheroption ::\par           'comment' quotedname        \par         | 'complete-label' quotedURL | 'full' quotedURL\par         | 'extension' '(' mand/opt quotedURL data* ')'\par mand/opt :: 'optional' | 'mandatory'\par data :: quoted-ISO-date | quotedURL\par         | number | quotedname | '(' data* ')'\par quoted-ISO-date :: '"'YYYY'.'MM'.'DD'T'hh':'mmStz'"'\par      based on the ISO 8601:1988 date and time standard, restricted\par      to the specific form described here:\par      YYYY :: four-digit year\par      MM :: two-digit month (01=January, etc.)\par      DD :: two-digit day of month (01 through 31)\par      hh :: two digits of hour (00 through 23) (am/pm NOT allowed)\par      mm :: two digits of minute (00 through 60)\par      S  :: sign of time zone offset from UTC ('+' or '-')\par      tz :: four digit amount of offset from UTC\par            (e.g., 1512 means 15 hours and 12 minutes)\par      For example, "1994.11.05T08:15-0500" is a valid quoted-ISO-date\par      denoting November 5, 1994, 8:15 am, US Eastern Standard Time\par      Note: The ISO standard allows considerably greater flexibility than that described here.  \par                PICS requires precisely the syntax described here -- neither the time nor the time zone \par                may be omitted, none of the alternate formats are permitted, and the punctuation must be as specified here.\par rating :: transmit-name number | transmit-name '(' multi-value* ')'\par multi-value :: number | number ':' number\par transmit-name :: transmit-name-char+ ['/' transmit-name]\par number :: [sign]unsignedint['.' [unsignedint]]\par sign :: '+' | '-'\par unsignedint :: [0-9]+\par quotedname :: '"' urlchar-or-space+ '"'\par alphanumpm :: 'A' | ... | 'Z' | 'a' | ... | 'z' | '0' | ... | '9' | sign\par transmit-name-char :: alphanumpm | '.' | '$' | ',' | ';' | ':' \par                 | '&' | '=' | '?' | '!' | '*' | '~' | '@'\par                 | '#' | '_' | '%' hex hex\par     Note: Use the "%" escape technique (% followed by the two hex digits that represent the character\par                in the ASCII character set) to insert single or double quotation marks or parentheses.\par urlchar :: transmit-name-char | '(' | ')'\par hex :: '0' | ... | '9' | 'A' | ... | 'F' | 'a' | ... | 'f'\par urlchar-or-space :: urlchar | ' '\par base64-string :: as defined in RFC-1521.\par service-error :: 'error' '(' 'request-denied' explanation* ')'\par                | 'error' 'service-unavailable'\par label-error :: 'error' '(' 'request-denied' [quotedURL explanation*] ')'\par              | 'error' '(' 'not-labeled' quotedURL* ')'\par explanation :: quotedname\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard\plain \s18\sb120\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Ref382644785}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382800936}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011357}Figure }{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\lang2057  SEQ Figure \\* ARABIC }}{\fldrslt {\lang1024 10}}}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Ref382644785}: Web page of a rated Web4Groups activity showing rated messages{\*\bkmkend _Toc382800936}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011357}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang2057\cgrid {\object\objemb\objw9135\objh11363{\*\objclass Word.Picture.6}{\*\objdata 01000100020000000f000000576f72642e506963747572652e3600000000000000000000100400d0cf11e0a1b11ae1000000000000000000000000000000003e000300feff0900060000000000000000000000050000000100000000000000001000000200000001000000feffffff000000000000000004000000a80000005b0100005c010000fffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffdffffff5a010000feffffff0602