00090200000000050000000102ffffff00030000001e00040000000701010082000000430f2000cc0000001700290000000300170029008600a300280000003000000017000000010001000000000000000000120b0000120b0000000000000000000000000000ffffff00ffffe3ff8fcf0000fff9f3ff8f0f0000fff071ffce4f0000fff831ffc89f0000fffe4dff873f0000ffff33fe0e7f0000ffffb8fd1eff0000ffffdc603cff0000ffffdf1ffcff0000ffffbffffeff0000ff007ffc7eff0000f01ffff83f7f0000e5e7eff81f7f0000edffc7e01f7f0000f3ffc7c00f7f0000fcffc7c00eff0000ff77c7800eff0000ffbfbf00097f0000ffdf430007bf0000ffdbbc003e7f0000ffb5bfffffff0000ffaebfffffff0000ff9f3fffffff0000040000002701ffff0300000000000300000000000000000000000000ff9f3fffffffffaebfffffffffb5bfffffffffdbbc003e7fffdf430007bfffbf000505006d6162656c00700078000a00000026060f000a00ffffffff000000000000040000002d01020008000000fa02050000000000ffffff00040000002d0100000e00000024030500b0008e00a7009400a7008e00a7008900b0008e0008000000fa0206000400000000000000040000002d010600030000001e000700000000}}{\fs20\lang2057 \par }{\lang2057 \par After having for example received five bulbs, the participant  should obtain  the right to make annotations to other contributions, or, after having received ten "hearts", he or she would  become "man or woman of the month", and find his or her face on the welcome page of that discussion. There are no limits to inventing new rating units and things that could happen after having received a certain amount of them.\par \par In general one could  distinguish between two forms of units depending on  the consequences of good ratings:\par \par }{\ul\lang2057 First}{\lang2057  there could be some }{\ul\lang2057 automatic relation}{\lang2057 :\par \par E.g. after having received ten light bulbs somebody would automatically get the right to make annotations to contributions of other members.\par \par }{\ul\lang2057 Second}{\lang2057  there could be }{\ul\lang2057 free choice}{\lang2057  among a set of additional rights:\par \par E.g. after having received ten bulbs somebody could  choose between the right to make annotations and the right to rearrange contributions, and so on. A logical consequence of this second form is that people start to trade rights and to evaluate them with rating units. A kind of economy emerges and rating units perform a function similar to money.\par \par }{\fs28\lang2057 \par }{\ul\lang2057 What}{\lang2057  could be traded?\par \par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 1. The idea to trade rights and "social functions" is somewhat problematic, because rights and functions should be distributed in a public sphere and should match the needs of the community, not just  personal preferences. Imagine that someone tried to buy the right to delete the contributions of others! Another counter argument is that the right to make annotations or the right to rate other contributions might be regarded more as a burden than a right - and who would buy a burden? Rights only become a real privilege when they are linked with a rise in social status, which  implies that there would be accompanying measures to establish that social status. \u8222\'e3Social status\ldblquote  in CMC is not pre-defined and is highly dependent on systems architecture and design. System designers must think carefully about how high or  low social status could be defined and expressed in order to make it interesting for a person to be active and to raise his or her  social status.\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\fi-284\li284\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 2. Other goods traded could be contributions. Somebody  may read what somebody else wrote, if he or she  paid  for it. This would  seem to be completely counter-productive, if one  wanted to stimulate the flow of communication. Nevertheless, in certain cases, it might be useful, e.g. if somebody wanted to get feedback from a person who  is well known for his/her competence. Suppose the competent persons would be  paid with three "pigs". What would s/he buy with that \u8222\'e3money\ldblquote ? There must be other services in the community that would  be interesting for him/her, like the advice of another competent person, e.g. a tax consultant, a translator, an editor, and so on. Such a system of exchange makes sense in rather big communities with a lot of different skills and experts. In such a context money may be a good medium to activate skills which  would not be activated without it . Money also seems to be a way of  regulating the demand for a good or a service, if  no other form of regulation can be found. The crucial question is where money should be used and where it should not.\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011269}Many Communication Environments Demand General Accounting Systems{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011269}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par }\pard\plain \s15\qj\li567\ri1133\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \u8222\'e3Essentially, communities may provide resources for the redress of infractions and forfeitures of debts that might not otherwise be redeemable. Social pressure from insult to incarceration to make good on all debts helps communities maintain the essential collective good of trust. The benefit of maintaining a generalized accounting system (one that allows for credit and does not demand intensive monitoring) is supported by experimental research (Kollock, 1992) in which it was found that generalized accounting systems yield much greater mutual benefit than tight systems that demand in kind exchanges at all turns.\ldblquote  (Smith, 1994)\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Small and closed communities seem to facilitate the maintenance of generalised accounting systems}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ generalized accounting system = \uc1\u8222\'e3one that allows for credit and does not demand intensive monitoring\ldblquote , that means that there is no system that monitors the equivalent between giving and taking.}}}{\lang2057 . Closure and well-defined boundaries seem to be a good way to maximise mutual benefits and to avoid free-riding. Families benefit from the fact that family members are more or less defined by birth or adoption. The fraternal communities in monasteries and convents are also based on well defined rules of incorporation of new members; common goods only belong to members of the community and not to outsiders. If outsiders may benefit is up to the community. It seems to be paradox, but outsiders may profit from the strong boundaries that keep them out if the protected community is able to produce a surplus, because of its special form of co-operation and if that surplus is given to the outsiders. By blurring the boundaries, outsiders would also lose something in the long term.\par \par In summary, there seems to exist a trade-off between boundaries and the need for money and other accounting systems: Strong boundaries allow mutual benefit in very generalised accounting systems. Weak boundaries increase the need for regulation and a rather strict accounting system.\par \par In fact a good deal of  experience gained  in CMC shows that open discussions with a large number of participants tend to lose quality and social coherence. On the other hand,  strong boundaries may not fit many conferences and discussions and may interfere with the open character of the Internet in many cases. \par \par \par \par }{\fs28\lang2057 \page \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505076}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505141}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505169}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505337}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373565155}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373651963}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373660856}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc381770874}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382827289}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828895}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896613}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011270}A Game Based on Rating{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505076}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505141}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505169}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505337}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373565155}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373651963}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373660856}{\*\bkmkend _Toc381770874}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382827289}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828895}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896613}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011270}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par To give the better impression of how the co-operation in rather complex systems may be designed, the following }{\i\lang2057 game}{\lang2057  was invented. The term }{\i\lang2057 game}{\lang2057  is used because of the game-like rules. Rules can be seen as automatic relations between the amount of rating units someone has collected and his/her role in the system. Please note that it is not a game in the sense of game theory, but in the sense of party games. Nevertheless it alludes to real social settings and mechanisms. The game introduced here is one of thousands of possible games that may be designed for communities, and its main objective is to discuss the impact of different rules on the development of communities. To enable a better understanding of the game it was embedded in  a real-life scenario:\par \par Imagine a trans-disciplinary research context, , for example "Artificial Intelligence"(AI) or "Human-Computer-Interaction"(HCI)}{\cs37\lang2057\super *{\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {* AI and HCI are typical examples of a "Mode 2 Knowledge Production" described by Gibbons et. al. to characterise a new form of knowledge production. "To summarize ... in Mode 1 problems are set and solved in a context governed by the, largely academic, interests of a specific community: By contrast, Mode 2 knowledge production is carried out in a context of application. Mode 1 is disciplinary while Mode 2 is trans-disiplinary. Mode 1 is characterized by homogeneity, Mode 2 by heterogeneneity. Organisationally, Mode 1 is hierarchical and tends to preserve its form, while Mode 2 is more heterarchical/?/ and transient. Each employs a different type of qualtity control. In comparison with Mode 1, Mode 2 is more socially accountable and reflexive. It includes a wider, more temporary and heterogenous set of practicioners, collaborating on a problem defined in a specific and localised context". (Gibbon et. al. 1994, S. 3)}}}{\lang2057 . In such contexts of knowledge production there is a rather rapid change of themes and opinion leaders. The role structure of a messaging-system should mirror that by allowing maximum flexibility in opening new discussions and in supporting a permanent flow of the members\u180\'ab degree of activity between being active and organising and being a rather passive consumer.\par \par In other words, a person's roles and social functions may vary greatly. There are periods of high activity, when someone is eager to co-operate intensively, and others  of lesser activity when someone prefers to lean back or  to retire. The assignment of real-life roles therefore is more or less 'soft' and fluent.\par \par The idea behind the following game is that teamwork very much depends on both a clear definition of roles and  on the members\u180\'ab flexibility to change roles and to perform other functions. Hierarchy and the will to remain "on top" mostly interfere with common goals or impede defining such goals. Therefore a game with dynamic hierarchies is introduced. A number of rules prevent persons remaining on top and therefore becoming exhausted and obstructing  others endowed with fresh forces. It may be assumed that people will accept rotation as something natural and positive for everybody.\par \par Players may hold three different roles shown in the figure below.\par \par }{\fs20\lang2057 {\pict\wmetafile8\picw5151\pich3528\picwgoal2920\pichgoal2000 \piccropr-80\piccropb-60 010009000003110100000500100000000000050000000b0200000000050000000c02640092000400000004010d00040000002e011800040000000201010005000000090200000000050000000102ffffff00030000001e0007000000fc020000000000000000040000002d01000008000000fa0206000100000000000000040000002d01010007000000180427001000200009000a00000026060f000a00ffffffff000000000000070000001804400010003900090007000000fc020100000000000000040000002d01020007000000180443001200370006000a00000026060f000a00ffffffff01000000000010000000fb02f4ff00000000000090010004000000000020417269616c000f2f040000002d0103000f0000002105110046696775726520323a20506c6179657273000a00080010000000fb02f6ff00000000000090010000000000000020417269616c000000040000002d01040004000000f001030009000000210506006e657762696525002200090000002105060066656c6c6f773f0022000e00000021051000646972656374696e672066656c6c6f7758002200070000001b0465009200150000000a00000026060f000a00ffffffff000000000000040000002d0100000700000018045a00100053000900040000002d0102000700000018045c001200500006000700000018045f0015004d0003000a00000026060f000a00ffffffff010000000000040000002701ffff0300000000009d5602003d000301781102003e000301b81b01004a0003014d200200c20100002701ffff0a00000026060f000a00ffffffff01000000000017000000210500004d6162656c2069732061206d656d62657220696e20612064697363757373006f6e201a0002001800000021052400616e6420696e2061206d61726b65742e0042757420746865206d65616e696e67206f662025000200180000002105240000}}{\lang2057 \par \par Newbies are newcomers that become normal fellows after a certain time (according to rules to be defined later.) Fellows and directing fellows match the two modes of activities described above. Members are to rotate between the roles of a normal fellow and of directing fellow. Figure 3 is to  give an impression on how newbies, fellows and directing fellows may be grouped into several discussions. \par \par }{\fs