g2057 \par }{\lang2057 Role 2:\tab One to three acorns may be awarded by the organiser. Every member starts with an acorn on his or her account. Awarding acorns affects the donor's account . \par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\ul\lang2057 \par }\pard\plain \s23\qj\fi-1100\li1120\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1024\cgrid {\lang2057 Rule 3:\tab After obtaining four acorns, a member becomes the organiser of the discussion. The old organiser returns to being a normal member, but keeps his account of acorns. By default, the founder of a discussion is the organiser and has three acorns.\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par So it needs at least one person\rquote s support to become an organiser: Three acorns from the old organiser and a person\rquote s own acorn are enough. An additional rule is needed which  will  make people spend their rating units.\par }\pard\plain \s23\qj\fi-1100\li1120\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1024\cgrid {\lang2057 \par Rule 4:\tab Organiser must spend an acorn every week. Normal members must spend an acorn every month. \par \par Rule 5:\tab Acorns that have not been spent are randomly distributed among people who have obeyed rule 4.\par \par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Rule 4 provides for the circulation of rating units. Rule 5 avoids an automatic decrease of rating units. If a person loses an acorn, another person must get one to keep the number of rating units constant.\par \par Please note that there is no boundary keeping unwanted and completely uninformed people out. A group of four new members are able to make one of them an organiser.}{\ul\lang2057 \par }{\lang2057 \par \sect }\sectd \pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\margtsxn1440\linex0\endnhere\sectdefaultcl {\header \pard\plain \s28\qj\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \tqr\tx9072\tqr\tx14034\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\f1\fs20 Appendix 3\tab }{\field{\*\fldinst {\cs38\f1\fs20  PAGE }}{\fldrslt {\cs38\f1\fs20\lang1024 103}}}{\f1\fs20 \par }}\pard\plain \s1\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\lang1031\kerning28\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011273}Appendix 2: User Groups\rquote  Scenarios of Voting and Rating{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828897}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896615}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011273}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\b\lang2057 \par }{\lang2057 The scenarios contributed by the participating user groups show a particular need for voting and rating functions in project management. Most of the scenarios presented below were extracted from a discussion forum which had been  set up by the Research Unit for Socio-economics using the software \lquote Hyper-News\rquote . From the beginning of the project, this forum was open to all participating parties but also to other interested people (\u8222\'e3friendly users\ldblquote ). Scenarios were continuously contributed and commented upon. They deal with possible uses of Web4Groups in different settings. The voting and rating functions are a response to only some of the needs expressed, such as  joint editing, multi-lingual support and annotation.\par \par In the following, the heading of each scenario names the author(s) and date of contribution. The scenarios are listed in chronological order. }{\b\lang2057 Highlighted}{\lang2057  text indicates parts which are particularly relevant to the question of voting and rating. Text in }{\i\lang2057 italics}{\lang2057  indicates additional information requested for clarification from the user groups. \par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \page {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382802096}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382827292}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828898}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896616}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011274}Vote on the Opening of a New Discussion Forum (Comune di Bologna){\*\bkmkend _Toc382802096}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382827292}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828898}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896616}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011274}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par Date: January 1997\par To: Early User Validation Report (Deliverable D5.1)\par From: Simona Valbonesi, Alberto Bizzini, Federica Bellagamba\par -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\par \par The Civic Network "Iperbole" is a realization of the Municipality of Bologna, that mantain an Internet server with informations, data and services about the City directly provided and renovated by the public offices and the associations involved in the project.\par Moreover, the Municipality offers to citizens and non-profit associations free access to the Internet world with e-mail address, and the possibility to interact with the Public Administration through e-mail and through the newsgroups of the hierarchy it.reticiviche.bologna.*, that are devoted to the discussion about city's arguments of interest.\par The aim of Bologna Municipality with Iperbole project is to facilitate the interaction between Public Adminstration and citizens through the uses of telematic instruments, such as the Internet infrastucture: that's why the Iperbole civic network is a pilot application in the Citycard Project, an European project (#8123) belonging to Esprit Program.\par In this scenario, the use of Usenet newsgroup assumes a particular relevance: infact the position of Bologna as a provider facilitates the entrance in the Usenet of many new users that are representative of all kind of "real" populations, and not only Internet veterans.\par \par Typology of newsgroups\par \par The Iperbole Project allows two kinds of newsgroups:\par \par - Promoted by the Municipality on collective and public utility essays, and on problems whose opinion about the Municipality wants to know, and that are decided to be object of discussion and confrontation on the Net;\par \par - Requested by system users ( citizens, associations, public and private firms ) on specific essays. }{\b\lang2057 For this kind of newsgroups are needed at least 20 votes for, counting out the votes against.}{\lang2057  Afterwards the reaching of the said number of votes, the proposal is examined by the people in charge of the service, to verify the consistency with general purposes of the Iperbole Project, and particularly the adherence to principles like respect of public interest, copyright laws and behaviour or language in no way derogative or offensive, or not apt to other users. In case of approvation, the new newsgroup will be set up as soon as possible.\par \par The requests that have been made, already visible on the Net, will be immediately processed as soon as they reach 20 effective votes for.\par \par If the demand for the opening of new newsgroups will exceed the capability of system management, it will be immediately communicated, and it will be necessary to allow for.\par \par There is the possibility to have three different tipologies of newsgroups:\par \par - free newsgroups, where everyone can post\par \par - moderate newsgroups, that require the presence of a moderator (a person who decide if an article can appear or not in the group)\par \par - particular groups that require a password to post an article into\par \par When the quorum for a new group is reached, the topic is submitted to the administrative service of Iperbole civic network, the Communication Service and Relationship with the citizens, and then discussed: this office decides if the proposal is compatible with the overall objectives of "telematic citizenship" of the project, and then opens the new group. Nowadays, only "free newsgroups" tipology is active, with 35 newsgroups.\par \par At the beginning Iperbole was a closed City Network and the groups where accessible only to the people who had an Iperbole account, but from May 1996 the newsgroups are accessible all over the world, and the Bologna hierarchy bologna.groupname of newsgroups belongs now to the most general hierarchy it.reticiviche.* , and the name of the domain is: it.reticiviche.bologna.groupname.\par \par To mantain opened a group, it is necessary that the group receive at least 35 new messages in a month, as the national rule for the hierarchy "it.reticiviche" states.\par \par (...)\par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \page {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382802097}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382827293}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828899}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896617}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011275}Project Management (MARI){\*\bkmkend _Toc382802097}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382827293}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828899}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896617}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011275}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par Date: Thu, 6 Feb 1997 23:30:00 +0000\par From: Glenn Bowen (glenn.bowen@mari.co.uk)\par ----------------------------------------------------------\par \par Scenario 1\par =======\par \par }{\b\lang2057 An EC project want to vote on changes to their workplan. The prime \par circulates an agenda to the other partners. This agenda has number of options which \par require a number of votes to be cast. All partners agree to simultaneously vote on \par these issues using a board set up for the purpose. For each issue the prime \par will distribute mail messages to each partner for them to send their reply. \par Replies will be of the form Yes/No , or, Option A/B/C/D. Replies will be \par sent to the board and will be visible to all in the group, showing the \par person who voted and their decision. when all the votes have been cast the \par Prime wipes the board and begins the next item for discussion. reports may \par be produced  automatically based on the way votes were cast.\par }{\lang2057 \par Scenario 2\par =========\par \par }{\b\lang2057 A closed discussion group wish to vote on whether to accept a new member \par (company A) to the discussion on a controversial subject.}{\lang2057  The group are \par split on whether to accept the new member. However, it is essential that all \par the group will be able to work with Company A if they are accepted in to the \par group. The vote therefore has to be secret .\par \par The members of the group are distributed across Europe and travel \par extensively, therefore the vote will be non-simultaneous. }{\b\lang2057 The prime decides \par to put a timescale on 1 week for the vote. sealed votes are cast. Only valid \par members of the group may vote. Only one vote each may be cast, and each vote \par is anonymous. the votes are sealed until the expiry date. Then members of \par the group may view the results }{\lang2057 ie\par \par Votes cast in favour if letting Company A join the group X\par Votes cast against letting Company A join the group Y\par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \page {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382802098}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382827294}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828900}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896618}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011276}Forest Development Plan (WSL){\*\bkmkend _Toc382802098}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382827294}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828900}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896618}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011276}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par Date: Thu, 13 Feb 1997 09:22:00 GMT \par From: Miklos Irmay (irmay@wsl.ch) \par ---------------------------------------------\par \par Mrs. Wood is working for "FORMACO", a specialised forest management and consultant\par enterprise. Among other tasks, she is actually the leading engineer of the forest development\par plan (FDP) of Pine-Tree-Valley region, Woodstock state in Timberland. This project was\par given to her company by Pine-Tree-Valley's 11 municipalities, owning 2 third of the region's\par forested land.\par \par The main objective of a FDP is to co-ordinate all interests, for e.g. economical, protectional,\par recreational and conservational, towards the region's forests, as well as to improve the\par economic situation of the forestry services and, if possible, of the woodprocessing\par enterprises. A further goal is to create a relevant collection of data about the region's forests\par for public relations and educational purpose. The project has to be finalised within 2 years.\par Mrs. Wood and her 2 collaborators, Mrs. Bush and Mr. Tree are forming the basic project\par team.\par \par Their task is very difficult and complex, because the elaboration and, to a much bigger extent,\par the consequences of the implementation of the results of a FDP are concerning the forest land\par owners, the responsible forestry service, the municipalities as well as different nature\par conservation or recreational activities oriented interest groups. The team has to prepare,\par distribute and maintain all documents concerning the project, lead and co-ordinate the\par working process, inform clearly and in an adequate way at every stage of the project,\par manage the many and sometimes very different opinions and interests as well as prepare all\par final decisions and the final report. They have to co-operate closely with the 11 local\par municipalities, each represented by 1 delegate, for they are the main actors in the process of\par decision-making and they will have to take over the responsibility of the financing and\par implementation of the FDP. They are also generally representing the local population.\par \par For the above mentioned reasons, Mrs. Wood and her team further decide to involve as\par much people as necessary to guarantee an overall well accepted result, but at the same time\par as few people as possible, to be able to push work at a reasonable speed, as the timetable is\par quite tight.\par \par Therefore Mrs. Wood doesn't forget to invite 2 delegates of the regional farmers\par organisation, as well as 2 committee members of the regions woodland owners organisation,\par which didn't exist before the beginning of this project and had to be created especially for this\par purpose. These two groups of people are the next concerned, because they own together the\par last third of the affected forests. Their main task is to participate in the decision-making\par process, but they are also invited to participate in the decision support process.\par \par To be able to trace back the region's forests history and for a better quality of the forestry\par relevant documents, generally to ensure not to forget some important information about the\par forests and also to have a control of coherence with existing forest and other relevant\par legislation, they invite the local forestry service representatives, that means the 3 forest\par engineers and 11 foresters, as well as Woodstock states forest management supervisor, Mr.\par Admin. They also will be involved in all decision support and a some decision-making\par process.\par \par Even some interest groups are officially invited to participate at certain stages of the project.\par Their task is, in a first step to give statements about their interests towards the forest and if\par possible to locate them. A further task is to closely follow the process of the creation of the\par mentioned forest development plan and based on that, }{\b\lang2057 to rate different solutions proposed \par by the project team, so it can prepare the propositions for the decision-making process.}{\lang2057  \par \par }{\i\lang2057 I imagine to have the possibility to chose for each proposition an \par integer out of a scale, e.g. which is going from "0" to "10".\par "0" to "4" would mean, that the persons/organisations, who are rating,\par "DO NOT AGREE" with the proposition, "5" means that they "ABSTAIN" from\par giving a rate and "6" to "10" means that they "DO AGREE".\par Giving just the choice "not agree", "abstain" and "agree" would not be\par enough, because no preferences between solutions falling into the same\par category could be expressed.\par The proposed solution is offering 2 possibilities to aggregate and\par present people's/organisation's ratings:\par         - just a simple count of "agree", "abstain" or "not agree"\par         - possibility to make an overall ranking, or a ranking within\par           the 2 main categories, to find the most preferred or unwanted\par           solution.\par }{\lang2057 \par }{\i\lang2057 I think a rating should be prepared and presented as a list, showing all\par propositions with short description or even just as a title or number.\par In any case the possibility should be given to click on a button leading\par to the detailed text. To each proposition there has to be the rating\par scale as well, where people can put their rating, e.g. by checking the\par corresponding box.\par Further a button opening a text (annotation) window would be useful to\par give people the possibility to explain their choice, if needed or wanted.\par \par Concerning the presentation of the result of such kind of a rating, I\par think, the result should be presented by giving the ranking, showing the\par percentage of "agree", "abstain" and "not agree" for each solution.\par }{\lang2057 \par In our example these concerned people are the 3 bosses of the valley's woodprocessing \par enterprises, as well as 1 member each of the local birdwatchers, the hunting association, the \par regional environmental protection group, a delegate of the local sports clubs (representing \par recreational organisations) and 2 teachers, very active in environmental education.\par \par All of them have theoretically constant full reading access to "FORMACO's"\par "Web4Groups"-server, where the whole documentation about the project is available.\par Unfortunately not all of them are connected to Internet. Those who have no "Web4Groups"\par access can be reached by postal mail, fax and/or phone. For this reason, the project team\par decided to regularly inform all of the concerned people about the progress by a monthly\par letter. But the use of "Web4Groups" don't replace the need to organise an information\par meeting every 4 month, nor does it help to avoid meetings out in the field to discuss the\par situation at the concerned objects. Everybody is invited to use the "Web4Groups"-terminal in\par "FORMACO's" office, where they will be introduced and helped by a member of the project\par team. For those connected to the server, the project team is opening for them a personal\par board and has created a FDP forum, moderated by Mrs. Tree.\par \par "Web4Groups" shows very clearly all metainformation and actions concerning a document:\par \par      creation with date, type and origin \par      editing actions: containing date, edited parts and their editor \par      annotations with date and author \par      keeps a log of the documents viewed, with the date and reader \par \par Nobody, except Mrs. Wood's team has edit or delete rights on the server. The 3 forest\par engineers and Mr. Admin have the possibility to make annotations as well as changes on the\par existing data (but only in co-operation with Mrs. Wood's team, who will give them limited\par and supervised access rights for those changes) to indicate actual changes caused by forestry\par enterprises interventions or natural disasters.\par \par The project phases are the following:\par \par After having fixed a project schedule, in the very first stage, an information collection has to\par be done. All kind of information like ownership situation, management plans, protected sites\par regulations, flower and animal inventories, water catchment sites and so one, in form of maps,\par databases, written documents, articles, legislation are brought together. Also some interviews\par with local people and a historical review, especially about known natural disasters, like\par avalanches, landslides, floods, rockfall, forest fires, insect calamities and storm damages,\par collected by a geography student, will be added. All these information has to be examined,\par sorted out, classified and made digitally available for the use in FORMACO's Geographic\par Information System ("ForGIS") for example. In this part of the project, the involved people\par first of all observe and read the incoming documentation. }{\b\lang2057 At the end of every month,\par everybody has the possibility to give ratings to the documents, by giving them labels \par from "not important", "important" to "very important".}{\lang2057  They can further participate by \par giving hints,  where more information may be found or provide them. They can also prepare \par new studies, if wanted. This phase is scheduled to use the first 9 month of the project. But it is \par an almost never ending task. During the first 18 month, by closing the decision support \par process, and well before the preparation of the final report, new relevant resources and results \par have to be taken into account.\par \par }{\i\lang2057 I think, that in this case, the rating result doesn't have to be made\par visible to everybody, as it is for the team's use. However, they must\par have the possibility to filter out the documents etc. often rated very\par important, e.g. sort them (automatically) by counting and accumulating\par the ratings they received.\par This is a kind of collaborative filtering.\par }{\lang2057 \par After this step, Mrs. Wood and her team, have to make the classification of the forested area\par into 4 main forest classes:\par \par      "Managed" forest with no management restrictions \par      Protection forest where management is restricted or even prescribed to ensure the\par      forest's protection abilities \par      Recreational forest where big impact from the (local) population is to be taken into\par      special consideration \par      Protected forests, where no management at all is allowed \par \par Parts of a forest can be part of more than one class. This classification is the heart of a FDP\par and is "politically" a rather difficult thing, because it will have a big impact on the possible use\par of parts of the forest. It is the base to decide, who is going to pay for the management of\par some special protection, conservation and recreation areas and the infrastructure serving\par these needs. It's also the base to decide, where management of the forest is restricted and\par owners are therefore hindered to do what they want with their forests and who is going to\par pay them compensation.\par \par On the upper slopes of the right side of Pine-Tree-Valley, farmers bring up every year their\par cattle and sheep to feed from mid June to late August. The forest suffers a lot from these\par animals (feeding on young plants and walking through the forest), so that no natural\par regeneration of the forest is assured. The environmental protection group also declared a\par particular interest for this area, because they found several very rare species of an orchid,\par which exists nowhere else. Therefore they want to protect big parts of the concerned forest.\par The hunting association is accused by the forestry service and the environmental protection\par group, that they don't regulate the number of game to a low enough level, that regeneration of\par the forest is possible without planting trees. The hunters accuse the farmers, that without their\par impact, the number of game would not harm regeneration.\par \par Fortunately it was still important, that the involved parties had to have a look on the site. This\par could not yet be delegated to "Web4Groups", although most of the discussion where held on\par the specially created "Web4Groups" "Orchid forest forum", so that also other interested\par people could follow the argumentation.\par \par After long discussions, Mrs. Wood and her team propose to solve the problem by creating a\par protected zone, to protect the rare plants and to build a fence around the most important\par sites, which has to be paid and erected by the farmers and the hunting association and\par maintained by the nature protection group.\par \par Another proposal comes from the teachers, who were following the discussion on the\par "Web4Groups"-server, and whose proposal is to feed the animals only every second summer\par in this area and to use another area, which was exploited by another farmer, who stopped\par farming a few years ago. This area, which belongs to one of the municipalities, is going to\par become forest again, which is not really desired from a landscape and environmental\par protectional point of view.\par \par }{\b\lang2057 Both proposals are in a first step rated by the involved parties and the final vote is made \par by the concerned municipalities and forest land owners.}{\lang2057 \par \par }{\i\lang2057 In this case the rating is thought as a vote without decisive character,\par as the people/organisations who are rating, have no decision-making rights.\par So this rating should first of all show the preferences for one of the 2\par propositions.\par \par If there is vote between two choices, a single vote is enough.\par A ranking-design is better for votes choosing out of more than 2 propositions.\par This is why both possibilities have to be possible.\par More complex solutions, like giving weights to some selected people's /\par organisation's votes, are too difficult and sometimes not well accepted,\par especially, if not well understood.\par }{\lang2057 \par Many more problems have to be solved in a similar way. The transparency, flexibility and the\par easy to understand tools of "Web4Groups" together with the excellent project management\par are leading after 23 month to the allover well accepted FDP and an extensive database about\par Pine-Tree-Valleys forests. \par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \page {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382802099}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382827295}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828901}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896619}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011277}Project Management (EFI){\*\bkmkend _Toc382802099}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382827295}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828901}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896619}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011277}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par Date: Fri, 14 Feb 1997 08:12:16 -100\par From: "Ivo Kupka" (kupka@rektorat.vsz.cz)\par -----------------------------------------------------\par \par EFI Scenario on Project Management\par \par 1. Announcement of a planned project using mailing list and faxes since not all EFI \par members have access to Internet. Most of them are in Europe but some are outside \par of Europe as well.\par 2. Creating moderated discussion space on the project plan where all EFI members \par can posted their opinions about the proposal. Each participant can see unread \par messages and can easily follow the discussion. He/she can sort messages by date and \par time or by sender or subject as well as by their combination. There can be personal \par messages, messages to all participants as well as fax messages sent by those who do \par not have an e-mail facility. There can be two kinds of conferences (discussion \par spaces): (i)  non moderated conferences (all participants have the same rights), and \par (ii) moderated ones where a chairman can influence the discussion and decided \par which messages can be posted and which not. }{\b\lang2057 All suggestions could go into the \par process of rating and voting.\par }{\lang2057 \par }{\i\lang2057 It depends on the moderator. S/he can chose the alternatives or offer \par all or some of them for voting. Moderator keeps in mind the main goal \par of the project and therefore he can decides on the main stream of \par alternatives.\par }{\lang2057 \par }{\b\lang2057 3. The final text of project proposal as well as participants should be the results of \par rating and voting process. The co-ordinator can decide about the rules of rating and \par voting and negotiates on the final text (in the case of unclear rating results).\par \par }{\i\lang2057 Basically there would be alternatives  or 'branches' of the \par organizational chart while starting and final points are fixed. The \par participants can vote for either one alternative either give \par different points to different alternatives (e.g. best 5, middle 3, \par still acceptable 1). The alternative with highest points will be \par chosen.\par If there will be too many participants in the project the same system \par could be applied on the final restricted number of participants.\par }{\lang2057 \par 4. Co-ordinator invites participants to the project and he creates a closed working \par space where only invited participants have an access to. There is still a strong need \par of fax access and communication tools for participants without an e-mail.\par 5. Joint editing of  the final project proposal ; all participants receive alerts about new \par comments or reviews of the text. The original text and proposed changes must be \par accurately followed.\par 6. All discussions are automatically archived under date, keywords and names of \par participants so one can easily find the searched comments.\par }{\b\lang2057 7. The important changes within the project proposal are subject of rating and voting.\par 8. The final version of project proposal is sent to the EFI Executive Board (EFI EB) \par and Scientific Advisory Board (EFI SAB) for rating and voting within their own \par restricted space.\par }{\lang2057 \par }{\i\lang2057 All EFI members can start or initiate new project proposal and it \par depends on the 'initiator' which procedure is chosen. But final \par decision is taken by EFI SAB and EFI EB. They will use the same \par system of voting and rating but with not so detailed \par alternatives, rather on the whole projects.\par }{\lang2057 \par 9. Project proposal and all relevant documents are available (only) for the participants, \par but some materials are available for all EFI members; general documents are \par available to everybody (a three level accesses to the documents).\par 10. After creating project management documents (time and task schedules), there is a \par follow up of the plan which shows an actual situation in the project management\par 11. Participants automatically get a warning about the incoming deadlines of their tasks, \par deliverables, milestones, reports and meetings within the project\par 12. There is an easy system to invite participants to workshops and other meetings and \par participants can easily confirm or cancel their participation in the meetings.\par 13. Different access rights to project documents can be easily created and updated \par (confidential documents sent to the co-ordinator).\par 14. There can be sub-coordinators in the project who can edit certain documents and/or \par add remarks on existing documents which will be visible or accessible only for a \par defined subgroup.\par }{\b\lang2057 15. All reports can be edited jointly and they can be subject of rating and voting.\par }{\lang2057 \par }{\i\lang2057 Co-ordinator could chose the above described system or adopt new rules \par but basically co-ordinator as responsible person should be able to \par drive  those processes under his own rules.\par }{\lang2057 \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \page {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382802100}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382827296}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828902}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896620}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011278}Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) Standards Development}{\cs37\lang2057\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\cs37\super \chftn }{ This scenario is not archieved at the HyperNews-page because it was only distributed via email.}}}{\lang2057 {\*\bkmkend _Toc382802100}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382827296}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828902}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896620}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011278}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 (Note: The IETF is not involved as a user group in the Web4Groups Project)\par \par Date: Thu, 20 Feb 1997 09:08:49 +0100\par To: Web4Groups user requirements mailing list <ratause@dsv.su.se>\par From: Jacob Palme <jpalme@dsv.su.se>\par -------------------------------------------------\par \par A typical IETF standards group discusses many small and large issues\par and makes decisions for each of them. The typical process for decision\par making without voting support is as follows:\par \par (a) Someone proposes some item to go into the standard.\par \par (b) If the proposal is liked by the editor, and no one has any\par opposition, it is just added into the working document and so goes\par into the final standard.\par \par (c) If there is controversy over the item, discussion starts and can\par sometimes be very active and lengthy, within the mailing list. At\par some stage, the chairman of the working group concludes that there\par is "rough consensus" on one choice, or that "this issue is so\par controversial that no consensus can be reched". The chairman often\par also says at that time "this issue is closed, no more discussion is\par allowed on this issue". Sometimes, the final decision is not taken\par until at or after the next IETF F2F meeting.\par \par (d) In actual decisions, the opinions of influential people may weigh\par more heavily than those of less influential people. Influential\par people are those who are known to have contributed valuably to\par standards work before, are accustomed to IETF principles, are well\par known and represents companies with important products in the area\par being standardised. This is not formally said in any document, but\par is a reality.\par \par The above procedure continues for many issues, often several in\par parallel. When the final draft is ready, it is sent out by the\par chairman for a "working group last call". At this time, anyone who\par has some opposition should voice that opposition. If no opposition,\par the document goes forward to the IESG (Internet\par Engineering Steering Group) which sends it out on a new last call,\par and then finally accepts it for publication as a Proposed or\par Experimental standard. Later, the standard may, usually after\par some revision, become a Draft standard and finally a Full standard.\par \par - - - -\par \par No "voting" is used in IETF except for (a) at F2F meetings, where\par trial votes occur to find out how controversial an issue is (b) at\par IESG meetings. In general, use of voting is not popular in IETF\par for the following reasons:\par \par (i) Voting may force people who have not considered the issue very\par carefully to cast a vote.\par \par (ii) The formal voting procedures used in ISO standards development\par are time consuming.\par \par (iii) The goal is to find a solution acceptable to everyone or\par almost everyone, not to find the most liked solution.\par \par - - - -\par \par Voting might be useful in IETF in some cases:\par \par (A) To avoid lengthy discussions on items where almost everyone\par agree.\par \par (B) To reduce the amount of discussion, since at present the only\par way to get your opinion heard is to write a message, and this\par causes a lot of unnecessary repetition.\par \par (C) To get rapid decisions on uncontroversial issues which\par otherwise tend to be forgotten.\par \par - - - -\par \par }{\b\lang2057 Here is a voting scenario for IETF:\par \par (1) Trial vote in a working group mailing list. Only the chairman\par is allowed to issue votes, but all members may propose votes to\par the chairman or to the whole group.\par \par (2) The chairman proposes a draft voting agenda. If there is no\par opposition,\par the chairman issues the vote a week later. No one is forced to\par vote, if the same person votes more than once, only the last\par vote is counted. The vote query most allow people to choose\par between at least "good", "acceptable" and "unacceptable", better\par might be to have a five level scale, "very good", "good",\par "acceptable"\par "bad but might be acceptable" and "unacceptable".\par \par Voting either ends at a certain date, stipulated in the voting\par agenda, or goes on continuously during a discussion (where\par people may change their minds by sending in a new vote,\par replacing their old vote). Vote results are open (i.e. the vote\par of each individual is shown), can be retrieved at any time\par during the voting process, and are sent out after the end.\par Vote results should probably not include any decision, just\par a good overview of the voting statistics.\par }{\lang2057 \sect }\sectd \pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\margtsxn1440\linex0\endnhere\sectdefaultcl \pard\plain \s1\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\lang1031\kerning28\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505055}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505120}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505159}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505327}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373565145}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373651953}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373660846}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828903}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896621}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011279}Appendix 3: A Review of Voting and Rating on the Internet{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505055}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505120}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505159}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505327}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373565145}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373651953}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373660846}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828903}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896621}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011279}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 (The following is the text of a document which can be found at http://www.soe.oeaw.ac.at/w4g/source/RateVoteSource.html). \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 It is suggested to view this document using a web browser in order to be able to directly follow the }{\ul\lang2057 underlined}{\lang2057  links.)\par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505057}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505122}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505161}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505329}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373565147}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373651955}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373660848}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382802102}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382827298}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828904}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896622}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011280}Simple Voting and Rating Services{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505057}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505122}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505161}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505329}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373565147}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373651955}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373660848}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382802102}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382827298}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828904}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896622}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011280}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Most of the common voting and rating services in the web (found as "simple queries" on\par the keywords Vote, Voting , Rate and Rating with }{\ul\lang2057 altavista}{\lang2057 , }{\ul\lang2057 lycos}{\lang2057  and }{\ul\lang2057 yahoo}{\lang2057 ) handle\par daily topics such as }{\ul\lang2057 Vote: Best Ice Cream}{\lang2057 , }{\ul\lang2057 Applets Rated by JARS}{\lang2057  and of\par course }{\ul\lang2057 Politics}{\lang2057 . \par \par Despite the contents and the pretty design of all these voting services, they have the big\par advantage to show an immediate response using diagrams, arrows, etc.. The big\par problem of these results are of course the identification of the voters and in the following\par the validity of the results. It seems that the voting-services-providers are aware of this\par fact, so they promote their results as a mixture of trend and public mood. \par \par Get an impression, what the net offers as "rating" and "voting" service today: \par \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\tx2835\adjustright {\ul\lang2057 Submitting a vote}{\lang2057 \tab http://b5.dal.net/b5/s/vote/humor/Koshs.Karaoke.Machine.txt\par }{\ul\lang2057 Votelink}{\lang2057 \tab http://www.votelink.com/\par }{\ul\lang2057 Plus/Minus Vote}{\lang2057 \tab http://www.dsiegel.com/damage/vote.html\par }{\ul\lang2057 Virtual Voting}{\lang2057 \tab http://alt.venus.co.uk/VOTE/welcome.html\par }{\ul\lang2057 Private Inquiry}{\lang2057 \tab http://web.mit.edu/dbrown/www/voteoj.html\par }{\ul\lang2057 Wave Rating}{\lang2057 \tab http://www.sirius.com/~bam/rating.html\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505058}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505123}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505162}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505330}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373565148}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373651956}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373660849}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382802103}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382827299}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828905}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896623}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011281}Advanced Rating Services{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505058}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505123}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505162}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505330}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373565148}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373651956}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373660849}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382802103}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382827299}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828905}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896623}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011281}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 As the amount of information electronically available increases, information filtering is\par becoming more and more important, and is being actively studied by researchers from\par many fields including Information Retrieval, Artificial Intelligence, User Interface, and\par Collaborative Systems. From these diverse fields researchers have developed a range of\par techniques to deal with information overload. We may categorise these techniques as\par keyword based, semantic based, and collaborative filtering. Of the three, collaborative\par filtering has the highest potential to detect the quality of a document because it taps a\par human element that is difficult to build into computer algorithms. \par \par Beyond these semi-automatic, user-driven rating services, we found numerous web sites\par that offer their own rating schemes on various products. Important rating schemes are\par e.g. the number of hits of a site, "Top 5% site" or a rating on a product given by a\par magazine. However, these ratings cannot be included in a search or filtering procedure. \par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505059}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505124}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011282}N{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505059}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505124}odes{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011282}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\fs22\lang2057 (}{\lang2057 http://www.gen.com/solutions/nodes/)\par \par }{\ul\lang2057 NODES}{\lang2057  stands for Network Operation and Design Engineering System. NODES is a\par system of software designed to facilitate the enhancement of human intelligence through\par collaborative ratings and a holistic interface to the Internet. It works on the principle of\par "collaborative ratings" and learns from your ratings and those of other people. Every time\par you log on to a NODES system, you will receive a list of new resources which NODES\par projects will be of interest to you. You will be able to rate these resources which\par improves the performance of NODES. If you wish, you can choose to be notified of\par resources your "list of advisors" recommends. \par The fundamental principle NODES is operating on is "individual satisfaction." Not just in\par the sense of "What do I currently view as 'quality' information?" but also in terms of\par long-term integrity. Will I continue to believe the information is quality? Will others\par continue to believe it is quality? What is the trend? Which direction are we heading?\par What are the constants in the equation? What values and ethics appear to be evolving\par into permanent attributes of our collective mind? NODES shall help us identify those\par "qualities" which are more permanent. For example, we may find that "authenticity" is a\par prized quality in individuals. Are you authentic? We may also find that "consistency" and\par "reliability" are transcendent qualities which receive and retain the highest ratings. Those\par who can be depended upon for "quality judgements" will be highly prized in the NODES\par network. A new society with new values, following a global ethic, will emerge as we\par become aware of "what works" best for everyone.\par Steve Moyer, who is promoting the NODES project, develops several ideas for\par application fields, with social and political relevant ideas, including alternative rating\par economies and exchanging incentives for persons, who get good ratings. \par \par NODES seems rather a project proposal than a software soon to be available. In July\par 1997, NODES offers only a mock-up interface and an extended technical description.\par The interface shall be implemented in Java. Connections shall be redirected using an\par Uniform Resource Indicator called nodes://..., replacing other URL requests. The status\par of the project is unclear. \par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505060}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505125}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011283}Firefly{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505060}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505125}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011283}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 (http//www.ffly.com)\par \par }{\ul\lang2057 firefly}{\lang2057  is a professional collaborative rating service focusing on topics like music, movies\par and artists. firefly is supposed to be an own free personal software agent. firefly puts one\par directly in touch with the entertainment which one would most enjoy and the people who\par one would most want to meet and talk to. firefly says to bring personalisation and\par community to the Internet.\par \par firefly is a spin off of the HOMR project based at the MIT, a music recommendation\par service. Agents, Inc. says, that in the first month 170.000 users signed up. Today, it\par offers several rating categories which in term makes the service interesting for placing\par advertisements, as the target group can be defined very precisely according to individual\par tastes, preferences and local areas. The service even includes a toll free telephone\par number for customer support. Usage of firefly, which is organised like a commercial\par online service, is free of charge. \par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505061}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505126}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011284}PICS{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505061}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505126}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011284}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 (http://www.w3.org/pub/WWW/PICS/)\par \par The }{\ul\lang2057 Platform for Internet Content Selection}{\lang2057  offers a protocol and rating specifications for\par labelling web pages, a service that can be offered by online providers that allows content\par specific pre-selection and access restrictions. PICS labels can be applied on any URL,\par including http, nntp or ftp. \par \par Many authors and web site operators offer materials that they realise will not be\par appropriate for all audiences. PICS encourages them to label their materials to make it\par easier for filtering software to block access. Thus, labelling a site will make it easier both\par for some audiences to avoid a site or documents and for others to find one. In\par September 1996, two }{\ul\lang2057 vocabularies for self-rating}{\lang2057  have been defined. Check out for\par PICS compliant }{\ul\lang2057 client software}{\lang2057  . Not many servers (or label bureaus) seem to support\par PICS yet; the main reason may be that PICS slows down browsing throng the net. \par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505062}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505127}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011285}Net Shepherd{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505062}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505127}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011285}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 (http://www.shepherd.net)\par \par "The Internet has always been self-regulating and special-interest-group oriented.\par Now the World Wide Web can be as well." \par \par }{\ul\lang2057 Internet Filtering Systems}{\lang2057 , Inc. (IFSI) announced }{\ul\lang2057 Net Shepherd}{\lang2057  in November 1995, a\par product designed to democratically rate and filter World Wide Web sites and selectively\par supervise access. \par \par Net Shepherd is said to be the first PICS-compliant rating and filtering solution. Under\par the auspices of The World Wide Web Consortium, PICS (Platform for Internet Content\par Selection) is a cross-industry working group whose goal is to facilitate the development\par of technologies to give users control over the kinds of material to which they and their\par children have access. \par \par IFSI's mission is to become the preferred and premier provider of Internet rating\par systems and services for individual consumers, concerned groups and associations, as\par well as other filter software developers. A World-Wide Web site currently contains\par basic information about IFSI, and offers client software for filtering and rating purposes}{\ul\lang2057 .\par }{\lang2057 A}{\ul\lang2057  white paper on collaborative filtering & rating}{\lang2057  is a good introduction and tackles many\par design issues. \par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505063}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505128}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011286}GroupLens{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505063}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505128}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011286}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 (http://www.cs.umn.edu/Research/GroupLens/)\par \par }{\ul\lang2057 GroupLens}{\lang2057  provides collaborative filtering tools for the Usenet News service and for the\par web. The GroupLens project, and the Usenet BetterBitBureau Service in particular, take\par the idea that the opinions of other people can help to find more easily messages that are\par similar to a persons interests and thus to make better decisions. The Better Bit Bureau\par collects each persons opinions (in the form of ratings) and correlates those opinions. The\par opinions are given to users in the form of predictions for each news message or for web\par pages. For ratings of Usenet messages, special news browser software is required.\par Transfer of ratings and predictions is described in an own protocol. In July 1996,\par GroupLens supports about a dozen newsgroups. }{\ul\lang2057 GroupLens for the web}{\lang2057  uses frames\par for collecting ratings and suggesting pages to visit. However, a brief test did not result in\par a successful rating procedure. \par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505065}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505130}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505163}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505331}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373565149}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373651957}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373660850}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382802104}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382827300}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828906}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896624}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011287}Polls and Elections on the Net{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505065}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505130}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505163}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505331}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373565149}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373651957}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373660850}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382802104}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382827300}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828906}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896624}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011287}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 There are serious strains, especially in the United States, to develop procedures for\par elections and polls over the net. Many political actors appreciate the net as "The\par medium" for a better }{\ul\lang2057 relationship}{\lang2057  between all political actors on all levels respectively for\par more direct democracy. For that these actors established several information-systems\par for political }{\ul\lang2057 communication}{\lang2057  and }{\ul\lang2057 participation}{\lang2057 . \par \par The official departments maintain own divisions for the development of new voting\par systems. The problems to be solved within this sector are Identification, Registration,\par Security and Validation. It seems that Identification and Registration for elections fits to\par the established American registration procedures and could be solved by merging existing\par databases or by involving established registration-Institutions (Qualified citizens may\par register in person at the office of their local election authority, by mail, at the driver's license fee office or at participating state agencies. Registration in person at the local election office is\par completed by filling out registration forms with information on identity, residence and\par qualifications which are signed and sworn to, then witnessed by the election authority or a\par designee. (RSMo 115.159 }{\ul\lang2057 Missouri Voting and Elections}{\lang2057 ) . \par \par Concerning Security and Validation, there are a lot of different systems in use at the\par moment, mainly on local (community and county-) level. \par \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505066}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505131}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011288}Selected URL's{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505066}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505131}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011288}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\tx3969\tx4820\adjustright {\ul\lang2057 Register to Vote\par }{\lang2057 (http://netvote96.mci.com/index.html)\par \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\tx3969\adjustright {\ul\lang2057 Florida Voter Registration Application\par }{\lang2057 (http://election.dos.state.fl.us/voterreg/regform.htm)\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\tx3969\tx4820\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }{\ul\lang2057 The MMP Electoral System (New Zealand)\par }{\lang2057 (http://www.govt.nz/elections/mmpfpp.html)\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505067}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc373505132}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011289}Direct Democracy{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505067}{\*\bkmkend _Toc373505132}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011289}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\tx3969\adjustright {\ul\lang2057 Direct Democracy with new media\par }{\lang2057 (http://www.vote.org/)\par \par }{\ul\lang2057 City of Cambridge, Massachusetts\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\tx1701\adjustright {\lang2057 (http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/iiip/Cambridge/prop-voting/prop.demo.html)\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\tx3969\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }{\ul\lang2057 The Center for Voting and Democracy\par }{\lang2057 (http://www.igc.apc.org/cvd/)\par \par }{\ul\lang2057 Elections: Results and Voting Systems\par }{\lang2057 (http://www.barnsdle.demon.co.uk/vote/vote.html)\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang2057 \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382802105}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382827301}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828907}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896625}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011290}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc382802105}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382827301}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828907}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896625}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011290}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 Voting services are not yet tightly knit to real political institutions. The rating\par services help persons to find easier the information they seek by collaborative\par filtering, but also rating results have no consequences for real social roles. Both,\par rating and voting services, may have very different goals: from entertainment to\par real world decision-making. When designing such services, it will be necessary\par to differentiate between these different goals.\par \par However, a combination of discussion, rating (for sorting), collaborative rating\par with prediction (for finding topics of interests) and finally with voting procedures\par of possibly very different sizes (members of a discussion up to a nation-wide\par public vote or poll) could offer important means for making the Internet a\par medium that supports decision-making for real (and virtual) world problems.\par \par The common problem of validation for voting can only be solved in a database\par assisted system (like Web4Groups), which allows identification or offers the\par submission of personal voting envelopes (keys). However, the voting data and\par the user data should be kept in separate baskets to guarantee a secret voting\par procedure. The Web4Groups Consortium should consider to trigger a special\par task for voter registration (by e-mail, phone or over the web), which could be\par part of the security task. \par \par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382827302}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828908}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896626}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011291}Rating by Telephone: Public Voice{\*\bkmkend _Toc382827302}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828908}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896626}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011291}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 The "PUBLIC VOICE Graetzltelefon" is an interactive telephone service that allows callers to listen to information by phone, retrieve fax documents or exchange messages on local topics. The discussion forum of this service has been specially designed for people who are not skilled in using interactive media; but  for frequent users special features like identification, browsing or rating have been made available. A field trial was  carried out in 1995 for four months under the research project "Political Participation by Telecommunication" in co-operation with the City Planning Office of Vienna (MA 18). The selected area was a suburban district of Vienna (Aspern). The purpose of the research project was to investigate chances and risks of  using telecommunications technology as a tool for enhancing local communication activities and decision-making processes. Several projects, based on kiosk, Internet or BBS technology, were  reviewed as well. One result of the comparison is that access to screen-based telecommunications media is not very widespread  yet. We estimated for 1995 that in central  European countries less than 2 % of the population  have private access to Internet services. The percentage was still below 10% in 1997. As Internet users are well educated, mainly male and have a  high income, they are not representative of the entire population/?/. So did users of the PUBLIC VOICE Graetzltelefon/?Sinn dieses Satzes/: a wide variety of inhabitants used the service not only for information retrieval, but also to  post  their opinions in  a public discussion forum. One can still dial in and listen to the voices after/?of/ the field trial (dial (+43 1) 589 30 22). \par \par A simple rating service was offered to callers of the Graetzltelefon. When listening to a message, people could give a rating by pressing a number between 1 and 5 to evaluate the contribution. New callers then had the chance to select only highly-rated messages. Care was taken so that users did  not rate their own messages using an identification scheme. For rating and other actions performable using the telephone keys or voice detection, special audio-icons were  designed. \par \par PUBLIC VOICE Labour is the spin-off organisation of this research project and today offers a wide variety of Audiotex services. In co-operation with "Radio Wien", further districts in Vienna will get local interactive telephone service. Services in Italy are in the planning stage.\par \par \sect }\sectd \pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\margtsxn1440\linex0\endnhere\sectdefaultcl {\header \pard\plain \s28\qj\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \tqr\tx9072\tqr\tx14034\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\f1\fs20 References\tab }{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\cs38\f1\fs20  PAGE }}{\fldrslt {\cs38\f1\fs20 109}}}{\f1\fs20 \par }}\pard\plain \s1\qj\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\lang1031\kerning28\cgrid {\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc382828909}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc382896627}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc384011292}References{\*\bkmkend _Toc382828909}{\*\bkmkend _Toc382896627}{\*\bkmkend _Toc384011292}\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 \par }\pard\plain \s36\fi-567\li567\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\lang1031\cgrid {\lang2057 a3eco 1996: Rating an der grossen Glocke\par Alton-Scheidl, Roland / Diallo, Samba / Tscherteu, Gernot 1996: Social Functions in Virtual Communities; in: Informatik Forum, Vol. 10, Nr. 